/","title":{"rendered":"Our Movie Heritage at 70"},"cta_label":"Next Article"},"nrplus_cta":{"enabled":false,"title":"Something to Consider","description":"<p>If you enjoyed this article, we have a proposition for you:\\u00a0<a href=\\"http:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\" data-saferedirecturl=\\"https:\\/\\/www.google.com\\/url?q=http:\\/\\/preprod.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1689865862247000&amp;usg=AOvVaw310hE1-m3o_FFzVsjI_hJA\\">Join NRPLUS<\\/a>. Members get all of our content (including the magazine), no paywalls or content meters, an advertising-minimal experience, and unique access to our writers and editors (through conference calls, social media groups, and more). And importantly, NRPLUS members help keep NR going.<\\/p>\\n<div><\\/div>\\n","description_mobile":"<p>If you enjoyed this article, we have a proposition for you:\\u00a0<a href=\\"http:\\/\\/preprod.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\" data-saferedirecturl=\\"https:\\/\\/www.google.com\\/url?q=http:\\/\\/preprod.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1689865862247000&amp;usg=AOvVaw310hE1-m3o_FFzVsjI_hJA\\">Join NRPLUS<\\/a>. Members get all of our content, no paywalls or content meters, ad-light reading, and community perks.<\\/p>\\n","button":"GET STARTED","button_link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe"},"timestamp":"<time class=\\"article-header__meta-pubdate separator\\" datetime=\\"2025-11-27T06:30:37-05:00\\" title=\\"November 27, 2025 6:30 AM\\">November 27, 2025 6:30 AM<\\/time>","queried_object":{"id":1897517,"date":"2025-11-27T06:30:37","date_gmt":"2025-11-27T11:30:37","guid":{"rendered":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/?p=1897517"},"modified":"2025-11-27T00:43:44","modified_gmt":"2025-11-27T05:43:44","slug":"a-norman-rockwell-thanksgiving","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/2025\\/11\\/a-norman-rockwell-thanksgiving\\/","title":{"rendered":"A Norman Rockwell Thanksgiving"},"content":{"rendered":"<p><span class=\\"drop\\">Y<\\/span><span class=\\"small_caps\\">e <\\/span>olde Arlington, where I live in southwestern Vermont, has, for a town of fewer than 2,500 people, abundant claims to fame. Many of the Green Mountain Boys lived here. Arlington was for a time capital of the Vermont Republic, taking it international, though with a population then in the hundreds, it must have been the world\\u2019s smallest. Robert Frost knew Arlington well, coaxed to live in neighboring Shaftsbury by Dorothy Canfield Fisher, Arlington\\u2019s culture queen, a Pulitzer-finalist writer, the pioneer in the Montessori method in America, and an assiduous networker. And then there\\u2019s Norman Rockwell (1894\\u20131978).<\\/p>\\n<Connatix preset=\\"loggedOut\\"><\\/Connatix>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-1-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-1\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-1-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>Rockwell, his wife, Mary, and their three sons lived in Arlington from 1938 to 1953, when they moved to Stockbridge in the Berkshires in western Massachusetts. Stockbridge got the museum \\u2014 the Norman Rockwell Museum, dedicated to American illustration and owning a trove of his work \\u2014 but Rockwell\\u2019s most creative years and his best models were here. I visit the Stockbridge museum once or twice a year because its exhibitions are always top-notch, and this year\\u2019s <em>Norman Rockwell: Home for the Holidays<\\/em> is a sentimental favorite. Rockwell\\u2019s many things, but I think his calling card is gratitude\\u2019s premier marketeer and image maker. In <em>Home for the Holidays<\\/em>, Thanksgiving stars but also Christmas, family reunions, country life, and tried-and-true values. Rockwell\\u2019s <em>Four Freedoms<\\/em> paintings from 1943, which led to illustrations in the <em>Saturday Evening Post<\\/em> and millions of war bond drive posters, are always on display in the museum and still pack a punch.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1897374\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1897374\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1897374 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?resize=527%2C307&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"527\\" height=\\"307\\" data-image-id=\\"1897374\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?resize=527%2C307&amp;ssl=1 527w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-art.jpg?w=1054&amp;ssl=1 1054w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 527px) 100vw, 527px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1897374\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\"><b>Left<\\/b>: Norman Rockwell, <i>Thanksgiving: Ye Glutton<\\/i>, 1923, cover illustration for <i>Life<\\/i>, November 22, 1923. (Norman Rockwell Museum Collection, museum purchase) <b>Right<\\/b>: Norman Rockwell, <i>Thanksgiving: Mother and Son Peeling Potatoes<\\/i>, 1945, cover illustration for <i>The Saturday Evening Post<\\/i>, November 24, 1945. (American Legion Post 193, Winchendon, Mass., \\u00a9 1945 SEPS: Curtis Licensing, Indianapolis, Ind., All rights reserved)<\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p><em>Home for Thanksgiving<\\/em> was the <em>Post<\\/em>\\u2019s cover image for its November 24, 1945, Thanksgiving issue. The models were, in fact, mother and son, Sarah Hagelberg, who owned a dairy farm in Arlington with her husband, the Rockwell family\\u2019s milkman, and Dick, who served in the 9th Army Air Corps, in which he flew 65 daytime bombing missions. Dick\\u2019s war is over and done. He\\u2019s happy to be in the warmth of his mother\\u2019s kitchen doing prosaic work rather than blowing Nazis to smithereens. Dick the model was only 26 in 1945. Rockwell aged him to show the toll war took but puts him in what could be a child\\u2019s chair in which the returning vet also seems happy.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-2-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-small\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-2-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-2\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit><TopStories variant=\\"default\\"><\\/TopStories>\\n<p>My parents knew Dick and his wife, Tish, through square dancing groups in Bennington County, where they also met Rockwell, a square dancing impresario. They owned a farm in nearby Stamford in Vermont so, while based in Connecticut, we were in Vermont a lot. Comparing <em>Home for Thanksgiving<\\/em> with <em>Thanksgiving Ye Glutton<\\/em>, which graced the cover of <em>Life<\\/em> magazine on November 22, 1923, is illuminating. Both were cover images, but <em>Life<\\/em> was by far more irreverent than the <em>Post<\\/em>, and 1923 and 1945 could not, as zeitgeists go, have been more different. <em>Ye Glutton<\\/em> is packed with mock colonial-era props and flips a stereotype by showing a beanpole as a glutton. The lightheartedness of the Roaring Twenties meets the peak of the Colonial Revival movement.<\\/p>\\n<p>For <em>Ye Glutton<\\/em> and nearly all his art from the Teens into the Thirties, when he moved to Arlington, Rockwell used professional models. Once in Vermont, he used the locals \\u2014 farmers, factory workers, the town barber, the town doctor, neighborhood kids \\u2014 for authenticity, some worn by age and labor, many with plain, sweet Yankee faces, some ruddy and robust from the outdoors. Vermont\\u2019s not grim, but it\\u2019s no place for slapstick. Then, people lived simply. Few were materialists. Winters were, and still are, tough. It\\u2019s isolated, and the mountains surrounding Arlington make it seem more so.<\\/p>\\n<Connatix preset=\\"legacy\\"><\\/Connatix>\\n<p>Rockwell gradually sheds frippery. The narratives he suggests turn simpler and deeper. <em>Home for Thanksgiving<\\/em> in 1945 could mean joy \\u2014 restrained since it\\u2019s Vermont \\u2014 in simple things like peeling potatoes or pathos since some never came home. Dorothy Fisher\\u2019s son, James, died months before the war ended, during the liberation of the Philippines. An Army doctor, he was killed by a mortar blast when treating soldiers wounded while freeing POWs from a Japanese-run prison camp. Fisher, nationally known as a novelist and public intellectual, lost her son. Sarah Hagelberg, a farmer\\u2019s wife whom no one knew beyond Arlington, got hers back.<\\/p>\\n<p><em>Home for Thanksgiving<\\/em> hung for years in the hallway at the American Legion Post in Winchendon in rural north-central Massachusetts, thought to have been a gift from Rockwell to a Roman Catholic priest friend who gave it to the Legion Post sometime in the 1950s to decorate its new building. This is, in itself, instructive. For visitors to the Rockwell Museum and any other museum that owns one, Rockwell\\u2019s paintings are bold, monumental, and arresting. He was a very, very good artist. Though scorned by academic snoots as an illustrator for commercial \\u2014 and bourgeois \\u2014 magazines and for the Boy Scouts, he\\u2019s part of the long line of American genre artists, specializing in scenes of everyday life, that runs from William Sidney Mount to George Caleb Bingham, Eastman Johnson, Homer, the Ash Can artists, and even Hopper.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1897373\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1897373\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1897373 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?resize=527%2C307&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"527\\" height=\\"307\\" data-image-id=\\"1897373\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?resize=527%2C307&amp;ssl=1 527w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-poster-and-art.jpg?w=1054&amp;ssl=1 1054w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 527px) 100vw, 527px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1897373\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\">From painting to poster.\\u00a0No one but in-house people saw the painting in 1943.\\u00a0Millions saw the poster. Norman Rockwell, <i>Freedom of Speech<\\/i>, 1943, oil on canvas. <cite>(\\u00a9 Norman Rockwell Museum, All Rights Reserved. Public domain\\/via Wikimedia)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>Rockwell\\u2019s paintings, though, weren\\u2019t the finished work of art. They were one step leading to the published illustration, often the cover image of a glossy magazine. Rockwell\\u2019s paintings would go to designers and printers \\u2014 for the <em>Post<\\/em> at the Curtis Publishing Company in Philadelphia \\u2014 and fashioned for both the far smaller scale of a magazine and mass production. The paintings, best seen as modellos, weren\\u2019t much valued until the 1980s. Rockwell gave some away to friends. His publisher kept many of them in storage. A few weeks ago, the Boy Scouts sold some to raise money for the organization\\u2019s child abuse settlements. For decades, the Legionnaires in ye olde Winchendon thought what hung in their hallway was a reproduction and nothing of value. They learned otherwise, to their shock and euphoria, when Heritage Auctions in Dallas sold it at its November 5, 2021, American art sale for $4.3 million. I suspect they served champagne and caviar along with their Thanksgiving turkey. And heaps of mashed potatoes.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-3-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit><MoreFromAuthor desktop=\\"true\\" variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreFromAuthor><MoreInTag variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreInTag>\\n<p><em>Home for the Holidays<\\/em> does Thanksgiving but also Christmas and New Year\\u2019s. It\\u2019s very fun and beautifully presented. Tiny Tim, Bob Cratchit, Christmas carolers, Santa, and the aftermath of a New Year\\u2019s Eve party are there. So is <em>The Discovery<\\/em> from 1956 in which a boy, shown aghast, discovers a Santa costume in his father\\u2019s dresser.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1897372\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1897372\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1897372 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?resize=527%2C307&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"527\\" height=\\"307\\" data-image-id=\\"1897372\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?resize=527%2C307&amp;ssl=1 527w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/arlington-bridge.jpg?w=1054&amp;ssl=1 1054w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 527px) 100vw, 527px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1897372\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\">Arlington isn\\u2019t exactly a hopping place. Here, a covered bridge and dirt road lead to Rockwell\\u2019s home in the background. <cite>(Kirkikis\\/Getty Images)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>Rockwell moved to Vermont to give his young sons a rural setting for clean, often crisp country air and for country values like love of the outdoors, neighborliness, and Vermonters\\u2019 well-known scorn for pretension. He\\u2019d lived in New York City and New Rochelle and tired of phony people and social climbers. Arlington wasn\\u2019t too far from the train station in Albany. The area had already attracted a group of illustrators, among them Mead Schaeffer, who\\u2019d eventually produce 46 <em>Saturday Evening Post<\\/em> covers. By the late Thirties, Rockwell was a star illustrator and seen as his day\\u2019s Charles Dana Gibson or James Montgomery Flagg. He bought a sprawling old farmhouse and land in West Arlington, steps from the old Methodist church, a covered bridge, and the Battenkill River, still one of the best spots in the Northeast for trout fishing. His neighbors owned a dairy farm. He loved it and planned to spend the rest of his life there but, in the early Fifties, he and his wife empty nesters, Mary in particular found so rural a setting too isolated. So, they moved to Stockbridge, about 70 miles south, still in the country but a summer resort spot for rich New Yorkers since the 19th century.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1897371\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1897371\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1897371 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?resize=527%2C307&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"527\\" height=\\"307\\" data-image-id=\\"1897371\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?resize=527%2C307&amp;ssl=1 527w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/rockwell-posters.jpg?w=1054&amp;ssl=1 1054w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 527px) 100vw, 527px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1897371\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\"><b>Left<\\/b>: Norman Rockwell, <i>Ours\\u2026 to Fight For \\u2013 Freedom from Want<\\/i>, 1943, Office of War Poster No. 45. <b>Right<\\/b>: Norman Rockwell, <i>Save Freedom of Worship<\\/i>, 1943, war-bonds poster. <cite>(Public domain\\/via Wikimedia)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>Steps from <em>Home for the Holidays<\\/em> are Rockwell\\u2019s <em>Four Freedoms<\\/em> paintings. The federal government commissioned the scenes to visualize President Roosevelt\\u2019s Four Freedoms that were the core of his 1941 State of the Union address and the Atlantic Charter. Freedom of speech and worship and freedom from want and fear were, for FDR, essential human rights to be universally protected. Rockwell was charged with developing images to enliven the four themes and found \\u201cfreedom from want\\u201d the easiest. It has to be the essential Thanksgiving scene as well as a view of family values and, to sourpusses today, a celebration of patriarchy, but who cares about them?<\\/p>\\n<ArticleNewsletter desktop=\\"true\\"><\\/ArticleNewsletter><AdUnit name=\\"incontent-4-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-small\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-3-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-3\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>By the Forties, Rockwell wasn\\u2019t painting before live models but, rather, used photographs and, no, he didn\\u2019t photograph an actual Thanksgiving dinner, though a roasted turkey hoisted by a woman who was indeed his family\\u2019s cook did indeed figure. Rockwell said years later that this was the only time he ate a model. He felt that the spontaneity he wanted, especially smiles and laughter, were too taxing for models to hold so he got them through photos and composed from there. He\\u2019s already choreographed a scene so knew, more or less, the poses he wanted.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-4-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-4\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>The cast of <em>Freedom from Want<\\/em> included some of his favorites. Jim Martin, who was a carpenter in Arlington, looks at us invitingly from the lower right-hand corner. He was a favorite Rockwell model and the only model to appear in all the <em>Four Freedoms<\\/em> pictures. Rockwell called him \\u201cthe old philosopher\\u201d in his autobiography for his unusual combination of gravity and friendliness. I knew Shirley Hoisington, the little girl peeking from behind the head of her brother, Bill. She was a teacher for years and then worked at our local chocolate shop. She died about ten years ago. Her brother was a real estate broker in nearby Bennington and died at 47 in the early Seventies. The woman holding the turkey was the Rockwell family cook. Her husband, Thaddeus, modeled for the standing man. Rockwell\\u2019s mother modeled for the white-haired woman whose profile we see on the right. Mary Rockwell\\u2019s opposite her, smiling. Florence Lindsey is the smiling, fetching young woman on the right. I met her daughters a few years ago at a Rockwell models reunion while I was working on an essay on the <em>Freedom of Speech<\\/em> picture. Rockwell\\u2019s child models, by the by, are still with us in surprising numbers, owing in part to our cold weather, which tends to refrigerate all things and defer decay.<\\/p>\\n<p>Is it sappy? Does it have the makings of a Hallmark card? Most of my academic chums think Rockwell\\u2019s work is fake and sentimental at best, science fiction at worst, but most live on a diet of angst and estrangement and, of course, few live in rural settings. Rockwell doesn\\u2019t do obscurity. He doesn\\u2019t do cryptic or crazy, either. That said, isn\\u2019t Thanksgiving a time when we at least <em>try<\\/em> to put black, disabling moods aside?<\\/p>\\n<ArticleNewsletter mobile=\\"true\\"><\\/ArticleNewsletter><AdUnit name=\\"incontent-5-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-1\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit><MoreFromAuthor mobile=\\"true\\" variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreFromAuthor>\\n<p>Rockwell thought the scene was a flop overseas. \\u201cThe Europeans sort of resented it because it wasn\\u2019t freedom from want,\\u201d Rockwell wrote in his autobiography, \\u201cit was overabundance, the table was so loaded with food.\\u201d But Europeans weren\\u2019t the target audience and, in any event, Arlington was a farm town where most food was what we\\u2019d call locally sourced. And how packed with food is this scene, exactly? Yes, the turkey\\u2019s a whopper and impossibly carried by the old lady \\u2014 the laws of gravity forbid it \\u2014 but aside from celery, some fruit, a plate of pickles, cranberry sauce, and a covered dish holding stuffing or mashed potatoes, it\\u2019s hardly the tasting menu at a Michelin-starred restaurant. Water\\u2019s served. Rockwell pushed himself in experimenting with a white-on-white palette and a complicated still life packed with geometric shapes and reflections. Light glowing from the window invests the scene with a touch of spirituality, and green floral wallpaper puts fertility in the mix.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1897370\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1897370\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1897370 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?resize=527%2C307&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"527\\" height=\\"307\\" data-image-id=\\"1897370\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?resize=527%2C307&amp;ssl=1 527w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/freedom-from-want-rockwell.jpg?w=1054&amp;ssl=1 1054w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 527px) 100vw, 527px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1897370\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\"><i>The Saturday Evening Post<\\/i>\\u2019s layout of <i>Freedom from Want<\\/i> and Carlos Bulosan\\u2019s essay in the March 6, 1943 issue. <a href=\\"https:\\/\\/www.saturdayeveningpost.com\\/2017\\/12\\/carlos-bulosans-freedom-want\\/\\">Bulosan\\u2019s full text essay from <i>The Saturday Evening Post<\\/i>.<\\/a> <cite>(Public domain\\/via Wikimedia)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>In 1943, the <em>Post<\\/em> commissioned four writers to compose a short essay accompanying each Rockwell <em>Freedom<\\/em> scene. Booth Tarkington, the author of <em>The Magnificent Ambersons<\\/em>, <em>Penrod<\\/em>, and <em>Alice Adams<\\/em>, wrote a story for <em>Freedom of Speech;<\\/em>\\u00a0the poet Steven Vincent Benet gave <em>Freedom from Fear<\\/em> context; and the historian Will Durant addressed <em>Freedom of Worship<\\/em>. All were famous then, but Carlos Bulosan (1913\\u20131956) was an outlier, a little-known poet, born in the Philippines, an immigrant, a labor organizer, and a socialist. The <em>Post<\\/em> might have been middlebrow but it was also a catholic publication and catered to those in the Big Tent. During the war, after ten years of the New Deal, this included socialists.<\\/p>\\n<p>\\u201cThe totalitarian nations hate democracy,\\u201d he wrote. \\u201cThey hate us because we ask for a definite guarantee of freedom of religion, freedom of expression, freedom from fear and want.\\u201d But Americans can never be free \\u201cunless we use what we produce,\\u201d but so long as people are denied the fruits of their labor, \\u201cso long will want manifest itself in a world of slaves.\\u201d With plenty to eat, he believes, we\\u2019re healthy enough to enjoy what we eat, to read, think, and debate, to be creative, and to embrace democracy as partners and players.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-5-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-6-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-2\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>Bulosan\\u2019s <em>Post<\\/em> essay launched him. Some critics compared his 1946 autobiographical novel <em>America Is in the Heart<\\/em> to <em>The Grapes of Wrath<\\/em> and a unique take on the plight of Filipinos in California. I haven\\u2019t read it but, judging from his <em>Post <\\/em>piece, he\\u2019s a spartan, bracing, graphic writer. It\\u2019s likely he was a utopian but, unlike today\\u2019s socialists, doesn\\u2019t hate the country. He wasn\\u2019t a nihilist, either. He was aspirational, fighting the good fight, and living like a monk. He did blather about \\u201ceach according to his abilities, each according to his needs\\u201d and was at the very least a crypto-communist, but I\\u2019ve seen worse. He died in poverty and was forgotten until his work and example were revived starting in the 1970s by keepers of the flame of Filipino-American heritage.<\\/p>\\n<p>When Rockwell read Bulosan\\u2019s draft essay, he offered to jettison <em>Freedom from Want<\\/em> and start from scratch. The two, he felt, weren\\u2019t aligned. One was about lack, the other about plenty. His editor convinced him that a general thematic unity was good enough and, by the way, the government was thinking of banning four-color printing to save the oil from ink. Better to go forward than lose time and be forced to go halftone.<\\/p>\\n<p>So, on this Thanksgiving, I\\u2019m grateful for too many things to detail, but I treasure my proximity to so much good art in so rural a place. The Bennington Museum, interpreting the art and history of Vermont, is a few miles down the road as is the Clark Art Institute with its princely collection of Renoirs, Homers, and Sargents. Williams College\\u2019s art museum is nearby, too, sadly closed as it is for a new museum building it really doesn\\u2019t need. Mass MoCA, the world\\u2019s biggest contemporary art museum, is in North Adams. Rockwell\\u2019s museum is farther south. I can\\u2019t call this an embarrassment of riches but a cherished blessing.<\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>The excellent Norman Rockwell Museum delivers a holiday feast of art.<\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"author":100,"featured_media":1897375,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"closed","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"hide-more-from-author":false,"disable-player":false,"disable-wfb-100-badge":false,"disable-wfb-100-archive-widget":false,"disable-wfb-100-legacy-widget":false,"hide_top_stories":false,"headline_variations":[],"coral_asset_id":"","_cr_original_post":"","_cr_replace_post_id":"","_cr_replacing_post_id":"","_links_to":"","_links_to_type":0,"original-print-title":"","hide-nrplus":"1","social":{"tweet":"","share-chart":false},"footnotes":""},"categories":[6917,6932,1290],"tags":[7109,2849],"section":[615],"coauthors":[5023],"class_list":["post-1897517","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-art","category-books-arts-manners","category-culture","tag-nrplus-member-articles","tag-thanksgiving","section-article"],"share_buttons":{"facebook":{"title":"Share on Facebook","href":"https:\\/\\/www.facebook.com\\/sharer\\/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nationalreview.com%2F2025%2F11%2Fa-norman-rockwell-thanksgiving%2F"},"twitter":{"title":"Share on Twitter","href":"https:\\/\\/twitter.com\\/intent\\/tweet?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nationalreview.com%2F2025%2F11%2Fa-norman-rockwell-thanksgiving%2F&text=A%20Norman%20Rockwell%20Thanksgiving"},"flipboard":{"title":"Share on Flipboard","href":"https:\\/\\/share.flipboard.com\\/bookmarklet\\/popout?v=2&title=A%20Norman%20Rockwell%20Thanksgiving&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nationalreview.com%2F2025%2F11%2Fa-norman-rockwell-thanksgiving%2F&utm_campaign=tools&utm_medium=article-share&utm_source=https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/"},"whatsapp":{"titl