lazy\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 253px) 100vw, 253px\\" decoding=\\"async\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2024\\/08\\/coffee_brass_icon-NLblock.png?w=253&amp;ssl=1 253w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2024\\/08\\/coffee_brass_icon-NLblock.png?resize=50%2C40&amp;ssl=1 50w\\" \\/>"},"source_url":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2024\\/08\\/coffee_brass_icon-NLblock.png"},"image_max_width_desktop":"175px","image_max_width_mobile":"150px","image_padding_desktop":"15px","image_padding_mobile":"10px"},"next_up":{"id":1896985,"link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/2025\\/11\\/ken-burns-is-completely-wrong-about-the-iroquois\\/","title":{"rendered":"Ken Burns Is Completely Wrong About the Iroquois"},"cta_label":"Next Article"},"nrplus_cta":{"enabled":false,"title":"Something to Consider","description":"<p>If you enjoyed this article, we have a proposition for you:\\u00a0<a href=\\"http:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\" data-saferedirecturl=\\"https:\\/\\/www.google.com\\/url?q=http:\\/\\/preprod.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1689865862247000&amp;usg=AOvVaw310hE1-m3o_FFzVsjI_hJA\\">Join NRPLUS<\\/a>. Members get all of our content (including the magazine), no paywalls or content meters, an advertising-minimal experience, and unique access to our writers and editors (through conference calls, social media groups, and more). And importantly, NRPLUS members help keep NR going.<\\/p>\\n<div><\\/div>\\n","description_mobile":"<p>If you enjoyed this article, we have a proposition for you:\\u00a0<a href=\\"http:\\/\\/preprod.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\" data-saferedirecturl=\\"https:\\/\\/www.google.com\\/url?q=http:\\/\\/preprod.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1689865862247000&amp;usg=AOvVaw310hE1-m3o_FFzVsjI_hJA\\">Join NRPLUS<\\/a>. Members get all of our content, no paywalls or content meters, ad-light reading, and community perks.<\\/p>\\n","button":"GET STARTED","button_link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe"},"timestamp":"<time class=\\"article-header__meta-pubdate separator\\" datetime=\\"2025-11-26T06:30:39-05:00\\" title=\\"November 26, 2025 6:30 AM\\">November 26, 2025 6:30 AM<\\/time>","queried_object":{"id":1897170,"date":"2025-11-26T06:30:39","date_gmt":"2025-11-26T11:30:39","guid":{"rendered":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/?p=1897170"},"modified":"2025-11-26T08:42:35","modified_gmt":"2025-11-26T13:42:35","slug":"our-movie-heritage-at-70","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/2025\\/11\\/our-movie-heritage-at-70\\/","title":{"rendered":"Our Movie Heritage at 70"},"content":{"rendered":"<p><span class=\\"drop\\">C<\\/span><span class=\\"small_caps\\">elebrating <span class=\\"small_caps\\">National Review<\\/span><\\/span>\\u2019s 70th anniversary should not neglect the art context of its appearance in the culture. Not to say that movies directly influenced founding editor William F. Buckley Jr.\\u2019s journalistic decisions, but it is important to know what specific social influences affect contemporary thinking. A look back at the films of 1955 is a perfect way to understand the magazine\\u2019s position and its influence, which helped shape conservative thought and language.<\\/p>\\n<Connatix preset=\\"loggedOut\\"><\\/Connatix>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-1-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-1\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-1-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>Post\\u2013World War II American film culture saw the impact of international cinema in the burgeoning art-house, foreign-language film theatrical distribution circuit, awakening public sentiment alongside nationwide responses to the era\\u2019s new political climate: the communist threat, the atomic bomb, and the rhythmic, visceral, cross-racial jolt of rock \\u2018n\\u2019 roll. Here are the films that helped American minds define individual conservatism in 1955 (and maybe now).<\\/p>\\n<p><strong><em>The Long Gray Line <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 John Ford personalizes the story of West Point through the eyes of Marty Maher (Tyrone Power), an Irish immigrant who spent 50 years at the institution. His rise from cook to noncommissioned officer, during both world wars, reflects how American identity was formed by duty, service, and, most amazingly, Maher\\u2019s realization that our experience with others defines our spiritual life, especially as Americans. Years after its release, scholar Tag Gallagher said of the film, \\u201cIn showing us what \\u2018made\\u2019 Eisenhower \\u2014 the picture enlightens us about what made us. West Point\\u2019s justification is that it <em>works<\\/em>.\\u201d<\\/p>\\n<p><strong><em>Journey to Italy <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 Roberto Rossellini\\u2019s travelogue through a troubled marriage (Ingrid Bergman and George Sanders) set the standard for future mores about romance, sexuality, faith, and mortality. Its frankness startled 1950s moviegoers and shaped the insight of successive generations dealing with everything from divorce to the Pill, mortality, and the existence of God.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-2-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-small\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p><strong><em>It\\u2019s Always Fair Weather <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 Gene Kelly and Stanley Donen\\u2019s clever, profound musical about three former GIs (Kelly, Dan Dailey, Michael Kidd) who reunite a decade after World War II\\u2019s end. Dancing and singing in ultrawide CinemaScope splendor, they confront the realities of American friendship and the Sartrean self.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-2-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-2\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit><TopStories variant=\\"default\\"><\\/TopStories>\\n<p><strong><em>The Night of the Hunter <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 Charles Laughton turned German Expressionist style into a children\\u2019s fable in this film, which features two orphans who elude a demonic killer (Robert Mitchum) then find protection from a God-fearing matriarch (Lillian Gish). It is a unique work of modern Americana about temptation and innocence, and a political allegory, if you like.<\\/p>\\n<p><strong><em>Summertime <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 David Lean\\u2019s intimate epic about an American spinster (Katharine Hepburn) whose trip to Venice becomes her sexual awakening. Her romance also replicates the West\\u2019s realization of its global circumstance \\u2014 a moral discovery that is both erotic and visually spectacular.<\\/p>\\n<p><strong><em>Ordet <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 This Carl Dreyer film (a.k.a. <em>The Word<\\/em>) contrasts a Danish family\\u2019s traditional belief with modern philosophy. The mundane tale\\u2019s simple purity is astonishing, and the climax is the most literally miraculous ending since Frank Borzage\\u2019s <em>7th Heaven<\\/em>.<\\/p>\\n<p><strong><em>Rebel Without a Cause <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 Nicholas Ray interprets the ascendant teenage generation\\u2019s identity crisis. James Dean in red, white, and blue jeans is misunderstood by everyone except Natalie Wood and Sal Mineo, who share his outcast yearnings. It was an instant prophetic myth.<\\/p>\\n<Connatix preset=\\"legacy\\"><\\/Connatix>\\n<p><strong><em>The Man with the Golden Arm <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 Otto Preminger uses a Hollywood exploitation premise about a drug-addicted musician (Frank Sinatra) to probe the social and psychological turmoil of urban America emerging from another war, this time in the culture and on the streets.<\\/p>\\n<p><strong><em>The Prisoner <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 Peter Glenville directs Alec Guinness as Cardinal J\\u00f3zsef Mindszenty, the Hungarian Roman Catholic martyr whose fate was previously depicted in Felix Feist\\u2019s 1950 <em>Guilty of Treason<\\/em>. This upscale version confirms the anti-Nazi, anti-communist tyranny that haunted the era\\u2019s political awareness.<\\/p>\\n<p><strong><em>Kiss Me Deadly <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 Robert Aldrich\\u2019s paranoid fantasy about crime and the bomb pushed the era\\u2019s social unease to such comic extremes that it should have finally finished off film noir style. Its shock \\u2014 the best Sam Fuller movie Fuller never directed \\u2014 was an awakening until Hollywood brought film noir back as a soporific.<\\/p>\\n<p><strong><em>Blackboard Jungle <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 Richard Brooks directs a fortuitous exploitation movie about an earnest teacher (Glenn Ford) dealing with high school juvenile delinquents. Brooks meant to come to terms with social and cultural changes in urban America but was just lucky enough to graft a pop tune over its opening credits and cast non-teenager Sidney Poitier to signify racial subtext with scene-stealing star power.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-3-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit><MoreFromAuthor desktop=\\"true\\" variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreFromAuthor><MoreInTag variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreInTag>\\n<p><strong><em>Marty <\\/em><\\/strong>\\u2014 Delbert Mann acknowledges the impact of media rival television by adapting Paddy Chayefsky\\u2019s sentimental pseudo-realist teleplay about a homely Bronx-Italian bachelor (Ernest Borgnine) finding a schoolmarm mate (Betsy Blair). Tearjerker sincerity takes the place of sophistication, but this was before Hollywood\\u2019s class condescension became insultingly obvious (i.e., <em>The Sopranos<\\/em> about New Jersey Italians).<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-3-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-3\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-4-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-4\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>These films \\u2014 as well as <em>The Deep Blue Sea<\\/em>,<em> To Catch a Thief<\\/em>,<em> Oklahoma!<\\/em>,<em> All That Heaven Allows<\\/em>, <em>The Man from Laramie<\\/em>,<em> Artists and Models<\\/em>,<em> The Far Country<\\/em>,<em> Bad Day at Black Rock<\\/em> \\u2014 plus their themes and issues, tell us about not only the world <span class=\\"small_caps\\">National Review<\\/span> first responded to but also the world we come from.<\\/p>\\n<p>Consider the little-known <em>A Man Called Peter<\\/em>, Henry Koster\\u2019s biopic about Peter Marshall (Richard Todd) who became chaplain of U.S. Senate in 1947. It takes a cooler tone than Koster\\u2019s earlier religious fare (<em>The Bishop\\u2019s Wife<\\/em>,<em> Come to the Stable<\\/em>). Call this the \\u201cGod and Man in Hollywood\\u201d genre, <em>pace<\\/em> Mr. Buckley. Not quite on the same level as Ford\\u2019s West Point epic, but by avoiding kitsch, it never insults a believer\\u2019s intelligence.<\\/p>\\n<p>From this retrospective, imagine what kind of publication could originate in the uncertain times of this year\\u2019s <em>One Battle After Another<\\/em>,<em> Sinners<\\/em>,<em> Frankenstein<\\/em>, and <em>Weapons<\\/em>. It would be unworthy of readers. To understand our politics, we need to know our cultural history.<\\/p>\\n<p><center><iframe loading=\\"lazy\\" title=\\"YouTube video player\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/www.youtube.com\\/embed\\/_SnzW8ziURY?si=g1L2D1aPVn4uO1rC\\" width=\\"560\\" height=\\"315\\" frameborder=\\"0\\" allowfullscreen=\\"allowfullscreen\\"><\\/iframe><\\/center><\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Recalling the film history that inspired <span style=\\"font-variant:small-caps\\">National Review<\\/span>.<\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"author":229,"featured_media":1897266,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"closed","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"hide-more-from-author":false,"disable-player":false,"disable-wfb-100-badge":false