/em><\\/span>\\u2019s John Daniel Davidson recently advanced a provocative theory, one summarized in the headline of his piece: \\u201c<a href=\\"https:\\/\\/thefederalist.com\\/2025\\/11\\/26\\/the-pilgrims-were-english-ethno-nationalists\\/\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\">The Pilgrims Were English Ethno-Nationalists<\\/a>.\\u201d<\\/p>\\n<Connatix preset=\\"loggedOut\\"><\\/Connatix>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-1-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-1\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-1-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>According to Davidson, the popular history you\\u2019ve learned about colonial New England is, if not inaccurate, at least \\u201cincomplete.\\u201d Yes, the early 17th century Puritans who fled from England were escaping religious persecution. But in Holland and America, the Congregationalist diaspora established for themselves a \\u201c<em>self-consciously<\\/em> English\\u201d \\u2014 indeed even \\u201c<em>hyper<\\/em>-English\\u201d \\u2014 social covenant. Davidson cites in support of this contention Cotton Mather\\u2019s trepidation over the prospect of \\u201cCriolian degeneracy\\u201d descending upon Massachusetts Bay. The colonists never gave up on participation in English politics, even English civil wars, and they embraced and promulgated English customs.<\\/p>\\n<p>And that\\u2019s why the notion of America as a \\u201ccreedal\\u201d nation is bunk, Davidson writes. The Puritan experience betrays the lie behind the notion that \\u201canyone, from any part of the world, can simply adopt and become American.\\u201d Thus, we should dispense with the childish notion that America is a product of the contributions of immigrants.<\\/p>\\n<p>That\\u2019s quite the argument \\u2014 one that uses \\u201cpilgrims\\u201d and \\u201cpuritans\\u201d interchangeably and in ways the subjects about whom Davidson writes would not recognize. But if we\\u2019re going to rely on Cotton Mather\\u2019s outlook to support the contention that the Puritans conceived of themselves as a homogenous ethnic sect as well as religious enterprise, we should be comprehensive about it. Davidson\\u2019s contention doesn\\u2019t sit well with the universality of the Puritan ideal.<\\/p>\\n<p>Membership in the Puritanical elect in Mather\\u2019s time was not a function of accidents of birth but of faith and ritual. In 1647, he sought to boost the ranks of \\u201cvisible saints\\u201d by advocating infant baptism \\u2014 a controversial proposition at a time when baptism was reserved for those who had and could expound upon their own conversion experience.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-2-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-small\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-2-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-2\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit><TopStories variant=\\"default\\"><\\/TopStories>\\n<p>It was a seminal transition, argues the scholar George McKenna in his absorbing 2007 book, <em>The Puritan Origins of American Patriotism<\\/em>. \\u201cLook, it was saying, we concede that many, maybe most, of our \\u2018visible saints\\u2019 are hypocrites,\\u201d McKenna summarized. \\u201cBut <em>our community <\\/em>is holy; it is bathed in grace, a grace that adheres to all of its members. That does not mean they will all be saved, but that they have a chance to be saved, for they are participants in the covenant \\u2014 they \\u2018and their seed.\\u2019\\u201d<\\/p>\\n<p>Which is to say that there <em>was <\\/em>a hereditary element to the propagation of Puritanism, but it was a function of your parents\\u2019 willingness to buy into the covenant. It could be entered into as well as born into. And if you adhered to the Puritan creed \\u2014 the evidence of which could be found in your observance of ascetism, the sweat on your brow, and the instrumentally useful works you left behind \\u2014 you could ascend to membership in the church (with all the accompanying benefits, like voting rights).<\\/p>\\n<p>This wasn\\u2019t just Mather\\u2019s outlook. The influential minister Peter Bulkeley adopted it, too. He cited the Jewish experience in the Old Testament, which, McKenna wrote, \\u201cmany Protestants considered a covenant of works,\\u201d and was \\u201c\\u2018in substance\\u2019 the same as the Christian covenant of grace.\\u201d In 1651, Bulkeley put his finger on the scale in favor of infant baptism because \\u201cGod conveys his blessings only by covenant.\\u201d<\\/p>\\n<Connatix preset=\\"legacy\\"><\\/Connatix>\\n<p>By the end of the 17th century, the Puritans themselves believed their way of life was coming undone. Indeed, they resented their fellow New Englanders for bringing down on them so many ills: King Philip\\u2019s War, the monarchical nullification of their colonial charter, and the disaggregating influence of commerce. \\u201cBy loathing of himself continually, and Being very sensible of his loathsome Circumstances, a Christian does what is very pleasing to Heavan,\\u201d Mather wrote in the effort to reconcile his contempt for what had become of a people he still believed were marked by God for great things. But the Puritans left behind a missionary legacy to which future generations of New Englanders would adhere.<\\/p>\\n<p>Mather, Bulkeley, and others crafted a social contract they believed had and should have universal application. In their gloom, these founding fathers of Puritanism probably could not have foreseen the intellectual fad that would emerge around the study of their republished works in the early-to-mid-19th century. They represented an origin myth that Harriet Beecher Stowe said would imbue in her the feeling that \\u201cthe very ground I trod on\\u201d is \\u201cconsecrated by some special dealing of God\\u2019s provenance.\\u201d Stowe\\u2019s staunch abolitionism was prevalent among the New Englanders who emerged from the Puritan experiment. Indeed, Stowe was preceded and succeeded by generations of New Englanders who committed themselves to the \\u201cNew Englandization of the nation\\u201d \\u2014 a project that produced tangible results even before the American Revolution.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-3-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit><MoreFromAuthor desktop=\\"true\\" variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreFromAuthor><MoreInTag variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreInTag>\\n<p>At the outset of American nationhood and in the throes of the Second Great Awakening, \\u201cwe see an erosion of existing theological differences between major Protestant denominations and a new style of preaching that puts emphasis on the spiritual brotherhood of all Americans,\\u201d McKenna noted. That was not a departure from the sermons delivered by Puritanism\\u2019s leading lights but the application of their teachings.<\\/p>\\n<p>The scholar Perry Miller famously described Puritanism as \\u201cnot merely a religious creed and a theology\\u201d but also \\u201ca program for society.\\u201d Our popular perceptions of what Puritanism was are shaped today by what it became. The stereotypical bluenose who is haunted by the \\u201cfear that someone, somewhere, may be happy,\\u201d as H.L. Mencken put it, is a caricature derived more from the Victorian ethos that prevailed in Mainline Protestant New England after the Puritans were gone. Likewise, the notion that the Puritans were fiercely protective of their ethnic homogeneity is more a product of the later 19th century, when \\u201ccultural pluralism was not in vogue\\u201d as McKenna observed. But the original Puritan ideal was as ecumenical as the progressive movement that arose from the ashes of the Puritan experiment.<\\/p>\\n<p>Davidson concludes by expressing gratitude for the Puritanical effort to preserve \\u201cEnglish folkways.\\u201d That is \\u201cour sacred patrimony,\\u201d he writes. Like his critique of the early colonial period, that story is not inaccurate, but it is incomplete.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-4-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-small\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>As Will Herberg put it in his 1995 book <em>Protestant \\u2014 Catholic \\u2014 Jew<\\/em>, \\u201cThe Puritan\\u2019s dream of a new \\u2018Israel\\u2019 and new \\u2018Promised Land\\u2019 in the New World, the \\u2018novus ordo seclorum\\u2019 on which the Great Seal of the United States reflect the perennial American conviction that in the New World a new beginning has been made, a new order of things established, vastly different and superior to the decadent institutions of the Old World.\\u201d<\\/p>\\n<p>He continued:<\\/p>\\n<blockquote><p>This conviction, emerging out of the earliest reality of American history, was continually nourished through the many decades of immigration into the present century by the residual hopes and expectations of immigrants \\u2026 and this conviction still remains pervasive in American life \\u2026 It is the secret of what outsiders must take to be the incredible self-righteousness of the American people, who tend to see the world divided into an innocent, virtuous America confronted with a corrupt, devious, and guileful Europe and Asia.<\\/p><\\/blockquote>\\n<p>It was the creed that drew those immigrants to America, and, to some degree, it was their expectation that we would live up to our ideals that compels the rest of us to confront our failings when we don\\u2019t. A belief in the universality of that liberal social covenant \\u2014 the enlightened American ideal that all men are made in their creator\\u2019s image and their rights are derived from God \\u2014 is the Puritans\\u2019 most lasting contribution to the American national character.<\\/p>\\n<p>If that\\u2019s ethno-nationalism now, it is a very strange sort.<\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Were the pilgrims really \\u2018ethno-nationalists\\u2019?<\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"author":194,"featured_media":1897762,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"closed","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"hide-more-from-author":false,"disable-player":false,"disable-wfb-100-badge":false,"disable-wfb-100-archive-widget":false,"disable-wfb-100-legacy-widget":false,"hide_top_stories":false,"headline_variations":[],"coral_asset_id":"","_cr_original_post":"","_cr_replace_post_id":"","_cr_replacing_post_id":"","_links_to":"","_links_to_type":0,"original-print-title":"","hide-nrplus":"1","social":{"tweet":"","share-chart":false},"footnotes":""},"categories":[1290,7508,5880],"tags":[2780,7365,9722,7109,2849],"section":[615],"coauthors":[4364],"class_list":["post-1897704","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-culture","category-history","category-religion","tag-american-history","tag-founding-fathers","tag-love-of-country","tag-nrplus-member-articles","tag-thanksgiving","section-article"],"share_buttons":{"facebook":{"title":"Share on Facebook","href":"https:\\/\\/www.facebook.com\\/sharer\\/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nationalreview.com%2F2025%2F11%2Fa-misreading-of-the-puritans%2F"},"twitter":{"title":"Share on Twitter","href":"https:\\/\\/twitter.com\\/intent\\/tweet?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nationalreview.com%2F2025%2F11%2Fa-misreading-of-the-puritans%2F&text=A%20Misreading%20of%20the%20Puritans"},"flipboard":{"title":"Share on Flipboard","href":"https:\\/\\/share.flipboard.com\\/bookmarklet\\/popout?v=2&title=A%20Misreading%20of%20the%20Puritans&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nationalreview.com%2F2025%2F11%2Fa-misreading-of-the-puritans%2F&utm_campaign=tools&utm_medium=article-share&utm_source=https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/"},"whatsapp":{"title":"Share on WhatsApp","href":"https:\\/\\/wa.me\\/?text=A%20Misreading%20of%20the%20Puritans%20https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nationalreview.com%2F2025%2F11%2Fa-misreading-of-the-puritans%2F"},"copylink":{"title":"Copy Link","