,"source_url":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/11\\/national-guard-dc.jpg"},"primary_category":{"id":5884,"count":14716,"description":"","link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/us\\/","name":"U.S.","slug":"us","taxonomy":"category","parent":0,"meta":{"blog-section":false,"section-blog-ads":[]},"is_capital_matters_archive":false,"specific_term_link":"<a href=\\"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/us\\/\\" class=\\"underline\\">U.S.<\\/a>"},"is_capital_matters":false,"is_wfb_100":false,"is_magazine_article":false,"is_premium_content":false}],"utm":"?utm_source=recirc-desktop&utm_medium=article&utm_campaign=river&utm_content=most-popular&utm_term=[INDEX]"},"newsletter":{"label":"Morning Jolt New","newsletter_override":"mj_sub, mj-optin-article-rr, nrpmf_sub","title":"Kickstart Your Day with The Morning Jolt","title_mobile":"Stay Ahead with The Morning Jolt","title_font_size":"1.9rem","title_line_height":"1.8rem","title_color":"#000000","title_color_header":"#000000","text":"Start your mornings with expert political insights from NR\\u2019s Jim Geraghty.","text_mobile":"Dive into the day\\u2019s top stories with Jim Geraghty\\u2019s expert analysis.","text_font_size":"1.05rem","text_line_height":"1.45rem","text_color":"#000000","text_color_header":"#000000","background_color":"#ffffff","background_color_header":"#ffffff","button_color":"#b37c29","button_border_color":"#ffffff","button_text_color":"#ffffff","button_color_header":"#b37c29","button_text_color_header":"#b37c29","border_width":"1px","border_color":"#d3d3d3","image":{"caption":"","credit":"","images":{"large":"<img width=\\"253\\" height=\\"202\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2024\\/08\\/coffee_brass_icon-NLblock.png?fit=253%2C202&amp;ssl=1\\" class=\\"attachment-large size-large\\" alt=\\"\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 253px) 100vw, 253px\\" decoding=\\"async\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2024\\/08\\/coffee_brass_icon-NLblock.png?w=253&amp;ssl=1 253w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2024\\/08\\/coffee_brass_icon-NLblock.png?resize=50%2C40&amp;ssl=1 50w\\" \\/>"},"source_url":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2024\\/08\\/coffee_brass_icon-NLblock.png"},"image_max_width_desktop":"175px","image_max_width_mobile":"150px","image_padding_desktop":"15px","image_padding_mobile":"10px"},"next_up":{"id":1896202,"link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/2025\\/11\\/the-dei-disconnect-between-actual-doctors-and-the-medical-elite\\/","title":{"rendered":"The DEI Disconnect Between Actual Doctors and the Medical Elite"},"cta_label":"Next Article"},"nrplus_cta":{"enabled":false,"title":"Something to Consider","description":"<p>If you enjoyed this article, we have a proposition for you:\\u00a0<a href=\\"http:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\" data-saferedirecturl=\\"https:\\/\\/www.google.com\\/url?q=http:\\/\\/preprod.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1689865862247000&amp;usg=AOvVaw310hE1-m3o_FFzVsjI_hJA\\">Join NRPLUS<\\/a>. Members get all of our content (including the magazine), no paywalls or content meters, an advertising-minimal experience, and unique access to our writers and editors (through conference calls, social media groups, and more). And importantly, NRPLUS members help keep NR going.<\\/p>\\n<div><\\/div>\\n","description_mobile":"<p>If you enjoyed this article, we have a proposition for you:\\u00a0<a href=\\"http:\\/\\/preprod.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\" data-saferedirecturl=\\"https:\\/\\/www.google.com\\/url?q=http:\\/\\/preprod.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1689865862247000&amp;usg=AOvVaw310hE1-m3o_FFzVsjI_hJA\\">Join NRPLUS<\\/a>. Members get all of our content, no paywalls or content meters, ad-light reading, and community perks.<\\/p>\\n","button":"GET STARTED","button_link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/subscribe"},"timestamp":"<time class=\\"article-header__meta-pubdate separator\\" datetime=\\"2025-11-24T06:37:55-05:00\\" title=\\"November 24, 2025 6:37 AM\\">November 24, 2025 6:37 AM<\\/time>","queried_object":{"id":1896610,"date":"2025-11-24T06:37:55","date_gmt":"2025-11-24T11:37:55","guid":{"rendered":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/?p=1896610"},"modified":"2025-11-24T09:26:10","modified_gmt":"2025-11-24T14:26:10","slug":"lets-hope-the-russia-ukraine-peace-proposal-improves","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/2025\\/11\\/lets-hope-the-russia-ukraine-peace-proposal-improves\\/","title":{"rendered":"Let\\u2019s Hope the Russia\\u2013Ukraine Peace Proposal Improves"},"content":{"rendered":"<p><span class=\\"drop\\">I<\\/span><span class=\\"small_caps\\">t\\u2019s<\\/span> been a confusing and dispiriting couple of days for the administration\\u2019s 28-point proposal to end the Russo-Ukrainian war. Who wrote it? Was it really a nonnegotiable demand on Ukraine? Now, there\\u2019s word that the U.S. and Ukraine have been working cooperatively on changes. That doesn\\u2019t make the original plan any better, but it suggests, one hopes, it\\u2019s a starting point rather than an inevitable destination.<\\/p>\\n<Connatix preset=\\"loggedOut\\"><\\/Connatix>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-1-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-1\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-1-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>The original plan tilts heavily toward Russia, and, worse, there are signs that some of its clumsier passages were crude, cut-and-paste translations from the original Russian. Impressions of a Russo-American diktat have been reinforced by the (since retracted) insistence that its terms were final, the imposition of a ludicrously tight deadline, and President Trump\\u2019s<a href=\\"https:\\/\\/truthsocial.com\\/@realDonaldTrump\\/posts\\/115599428464496784\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\">\\u00a0petulant grumbling<\\/a>\\u00a0on Truth Social about Ukrainian \\u201cingratitude.\\u201d<\\/p>\\n<p>What the president may have seen as necessary toughness struck many as a display of both weakness and na\\u00efvet\\u00e9. Vladimir Putin has repeatedly played Trump for a fool, and it\\u2019s his goal to keep doing so.<\\/p>\\n<p>Regarding the 28 points, the first \\u2014 that Ukraine\\u2019s sovereignty will be \\u201cconfirmed\\u201d \\u2014 is worth one cheer, but falls well short of the standard set in the <a href=\\"https:\\/\\/www.brookings.edu\\/articles\\/why-care-about-ukraine-and-the-budapest-memorandum\\/\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\">1994 Budapest Memorandum<\\/a>. Back then, Russia agreed \\u201cto respect the independence and sovereignty\\u00a0<em>and the existing borders of Ukraine<\\/em><i><span style=\\"font-weight: normal !msorm;\\">\\u201d<\\/span><\\/i> (our emphasis). That promise died in the Donbas and Crimea in 2014. To restate that pledge, if only partially, would be a start, but not a reassuring one.<\\/p>\\n<p>Ukraine must now realize that treaties count for less than force. Despite the remarkable efforts of the Ukrainian military, Russia is using its superior resources (and behind them, those of China) to grind Ukraine down. With the Kremlin\\u2019s grip secure at home, Russia is not going to lose this war on anything like the current trajectory, nor will it, with extremely limited exceptions, give up any of the territory that it has conquered. We wish it were otherwise, but it is not. The only question now for Kyiv has to be the preservation of the independence of what is left (the\\u00a0<a href=\\"https:\\/\\/www.reuters.com\\/business\\/finance\\/full-text-european-counter-proposal-us-ukraine-peace-plan-2025-11-23\\/\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\">rival European plan<\\/a>\\u00a0would be a far better option to achieve that end, but there is no chance that Russia would accept it).<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-2-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-small\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-2-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-2\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit><TopStories variant=\\"default\\"><\\/TopStories>\\n<p>The backdrop of all the diplomacy\\u00a0should be the recognition that Russia\\u2019s promises are worthless. As the last decade has shown, the financial \\u201cincentives\\u201d for its good behavior would make no difference if the Kremlin decided that a renewed onslaught on Ukraine would be in Russia\\u2019s national interest. Moreover, many of the 28 points are so broad that, even if incorporated in a properly drafted treaty, it would be easy enough for Russia to claim some breach and go back on the offensive.<\\/p>\\n<p>All that said, there may be enough in the initial document to work toward a deal that could be the basis of a sustainable armistice, which, in the most optimistic scenario, may be afoot now.<\\/p>\\n<p>For example, one of its proposals is that the size of Ukraine\\u2019s miliary should be capped. That\\u2019s insulting, but bearable, although the proposed number (600,000) is too low. Currently, Ukraine has around\\u00a0<a href=\\"https:\\/\\/www.cfr.org\\/in-brief\\/comparing-size-and-capabilities-russian-and-ukrainian-militaries\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\">900,000 active-duty personnel<\\/a>. That should be the lowest acceptable limit.<\\/p>\\n<Connatix preset=\\"legacy\\"><\\/Connatix>\\n<p>Then there is a requirement that Ukraine should not join NATO. While there is no chance that Ukraine will join NATO any time soon, a fully sovereign Ukraine should be able to ally with whomever it wants. A period of, say, five years in which it agrees to suspend its current application ought to be acceptable.<\\/p>\\n<p>The provision that NATO troops should not be stationed in Ukraine is tolerable, so long as it is clear that the prohibition only covers permanent bases. It would be worth floating the idea that similar limits should apply to the stationing of Russian troops in Belarus.<\\/p>\\n<p>The recognition that \\u201cEuropean fighter jets\\u201d may be stationed in Poland (they already are) must not be read as excluding U.S. planes \\u2014 there can be no second-class citizens within NATO.<\\/p>\\n<p>Meanwhile, the agreement that Ukraine is free to join the EU ought to be unnecessary if Ukrainian sovereignty is to mean anything, but this provision is nevertheless welcome.<\\/p>\\n<p>And then there are the territorial issues. De facto recognition (including by the U.S.) of the Russian occupation of Crimea and the Luhansk Oblast is an unpleasant acknowledgement of reality, as is de facto recognition of the \\u201cborder\\u201d established by the front line in Kherson and Zaporizhzhia. Russia is prepared to withdraw from other Ukrainian territory it has seized, with the exception of the Donetsk Oblast. There, it wants de facto recognition of what it has already taken, which is, under these bleak circumstances, acceptable. But the surrender to Moscow of those parts of the oblast \\u2014 a key defensive line \\u2014 that Russia has not taken and their establishment as a Russian \\u201cDMV\\u201d is not. The Ukrainian fortifications block the most likely route that Russia would take in any\\u00a0<a href=\\"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/2025\\/11\\/the-terms-of-ukraines-defeat-and-ours\\/?utm_source=recirc-desktop&amp;utm_medium=homepage&amp;utm_campaign=hero&amp;utm_content=related&amp;utm_term=first\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\">renewed attempt to subdue<\\/a> Ukraine.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-3-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit><MoreFromAuthor desktop=\\"true\\" variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreFromAuthor><MoreInTag variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreInTag>\\n<p>We hope further negotiations are addressing the points above, but not only those. There\\u2019s the issue of spelling out what point five (\\u201cUkraine will receive reliable security guarantees\\u201d) is to mean. Unless those guarantees are truly \\u201creliable,\\u201d no armistice will last for long. The best chance of persuading Moscow to accept them will be for the administration to be clear that, in the absence of an agreement, it will continue to support Ukraine\\u2019s fight for self-determination.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-3-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-3\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>The alternative of pressing for a pseudo peace acceptable in practice only to Moscow would likely be a catastrophe with consequences that would be felt well beyond Ukraine, and not just in Europe. Xi will be watching and taking notes, and so will the governments of many other countries, weighing which way to leap as the world divides into two camps. And those governments may include some currently aligned with the U.S. who might well conclude that they should at least start hedging their bets.<\\/p>\\n<p>That would not be in their interests, and it would not be in ours either.<\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Pressing for a pseudo peace acceptable in practice only to Moscow would likely be a catastrophe with consequences that would be felt well beyond Ukraine.<\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"author":149,"featured_media":1896618,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"closed","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"hide-more-from-author":false,"disable-player":false,"disable-wfb-100-badge":false,"disable-wfb-100-archive-widget":false,"disable-wfb-100-legacy-widget":false,"hide_top_stories":false,"headline_variations":[],"coral_asset_id":"","_cr_original_post":"","_cr_replace_post_id":"","_cr_replacing_post_id":"","_links_to":"","_links_to_type":0,"original-print-title":"","hi