769d8b33-f983-4de4-b150-b8fb886d7b87	https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2025/12/01/congress/thune-casts-doubt-on-passing-government-funding-bills-before-christmas-00671973	\N	dc72f70d-809a-42c1-b218-b2ed57239432	Thune casts doubt on passing government funding bills before Christmas	Senate Majority Leader John Thune acknowledged Monday that his chamber might not have time this month to pass another package of government funding bills, casting doubt on the prospects for a year-end deal to avert another shutdown.\n\nCongress is back. Welcome to the December of Hellth.\n\nLawmakers now have a mere 30 days to address expiring Obamacare tax credits and prevent health insurance hikes for millions of Americans. Republicans and Democrats agree success hangs on one question: Will President Donald Trump figure out what he wants?\n\nInterpreting Trump's intentions has gotten tougher since lawmakers left Washington. Early in the break, Trump appeared to be on the brink of announcing a framework to extend the Affordable Care Act subsidies with new eligibility restrictions, only to pull back after GOP criticism. He then said he doesn't want to extend the subsidies but understands it might be necessary.\n\nCapitol Hill factions are trying to figure out what Trump wants and how to entice him to their side.\n\n'The president has got to sign whatever we do, otherwise it's a legislative exercise,' says Rep. Brian Fitzpatrick, the Pennsylvania Republican who is drafting what he describes as a bipartisan proposal that would largely align with last week's leaked White House framework.\n\nThe timeline as we know it — Though the ACA tax credits expire at month's end, some lawmakers are looking at Jan. 30, the next shutdown deadline, as the real cutoff for a health care deal.\n\nThe Senate will vote as soon as Dec. 9 on an undefined health care proposal, the fulfillment of the bipartisan deal to reopen the government.\n\nWhat's in the works — Fitzpatrick and other centrists are looking to build bipartisan support for an extension of the subsidies with new income restrictions and other safeguards. Their efforts have loose backing from the Republican Main Street Caucus, whose chair Rep. Mike Flood (Neb.) endorsed the contours of the leaked White House health plan.\n\nA coalition of House and Senate Republicans that includes key committee chairs are working behind the scenes on a range of possible proposals, but there's no guarantee the GOP will fall in line or the lawmakers will produce a bill this year.\n\nSenate Finance Chair Mike Crapo (R-Idaho) says he is 'working to try to find a pathway to get some bipartisan solution' — not a budget reconciliation bill.\n\nAs a fail-safe, centrist House Republicans are prepared to launch a discharge petition to force a floor vote on a subsidy extension. But they're also trying to give space to the Senate to see if a bipartisan deal can be reached.\n\nDemocrats have yet to reach a consensus. A group of Democrats including Sen. Jeanne Shaheen (N.H.) and Rep. Tom Suozzi (N.Y.) has been in close contact with Republicans including Fitzpatrick and Sen. Lisa Murkowski (Alaska) about what could get bipartisan support. But others, including independent Sen. Bernie Sanders of Vermont, want Democrats to embrace a sweeping health care plan that could be a rallying cry for the midterms but has no chance of winning GOP votes.\n\nThere's widespread skepticism that Republicans will agree to any plan that isn't fully endorsed by Trump. A significant swath of GOP lawmakers will simply never vote to extend anything related to Obamacare, according to three GOP aides granted anonymity to discuss internal dynamics.\n\n'That's the trouble today,' says Sen. Chris Murphy (D-Conn.). 'You can have good-faith negotiations with Republicans, but it just doesn't matter until Donald Trump weighs in.'\n\nWhat else we're watching:\n— What's next for appropriations: House GOP appropriators aren't yet ready to advance the two biggest outstanding funding bills — Defense and Labor-HHS-Education — and are instead rooting for a cross-chamber compromise on several smaller funding measures, with sights on enactment by month's end.\n\nSenate Republicans are also looking for a smaller group of bills that could get signed into law as they continue to pursue a separate minibus that would pair defense funding with Labor-HHS-Education and other bills, according to two aides granted anonymity to disclose internal deliberations.\n\nAny appropriations bills finalized in the next three weeks would be ripe for hitching to a year-end agreement on health insurance subsidies, should one materialize.\n\n— NDAA deal incoming: Lawmakers plan to release the final text of a compromise version of the National Defense Authorization Act this week and tee up a House floor vote as soon as next week, though House and Senate leaders still need to sign off on the final product.\n\nJordain Carney, Jennifer Scholtes, Katherine Tully-McManus and Connor O'Brien contributed to this report.	Senate Majority Leader John Thune acknowledged Monday that his chamber might not have time this month to pass another package of government funding bills, casting doubt on the prospects for a year-end deal to avert another shutdown.	2025-12-01 00:00:00	2025-12-02 14:03:47.432	2025-12-02 14:03:47.432	2025-12-02 14:03:47.432
228f16ba-8cd5-49e2-a843-ac7ab8ff928a	https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2025/11/12/congress/trump-signs-bill-ending-longest-government-shutdown-in-us-history-00650189	\N	dc72f70d-809a-42c1-b218-b2ed57239432	Trump signs bill ending longest government shutdown in US history	President Donald Trump signed legislation late Wednesday to end the government shutdown that spanned 43 days, punting the next funding deadline to January 30 and leaving Congress with a daunting to-do list for the new year.\n\nThe bill, which passed both chambers with bipartisan support, funds the government through the end of January and provides a temporary reprieve for federal workers and agencies affected by the shutdown. The measure also includes a provision to extend the current funding levels for defense and non-defense discretionary spending, but does not resolve the underlying disputes over border security and immigration policy that triggered the shutdown.\n\nSenate Majority Leader John Thune (R-S.D.) hailed the bill as a necessary step to avoid further economic disruption and to allow lawmakers time to negotiate a longer-term solution. "This is not the end of the story," Thune said in a statement. "We still have a lot of work to do to address the issues that led to this shutdown and to ensure that our government can function effectively in the future."\n\nHouse Speaker Mike Johnson (R-La.) echoed Thune's remarks, saying that the bill "gives us the breathing room we need to work on a comprehensive solution that will finally put an end to these recurring shutdowns."\n\nThe shutdown, which began in late October, was the longest in U.S. history and had a significant impact on federal workers, contractors, and the broader economy. According to a report by the Congressional Budget Office, the shutdown cost the economy an estimated $11 billion and resulted in the loss of tens of thousands of jobs.\n\nThe bill also includes a provision to restore back pay for federal workers who were furloughed during the shutdown, as well as funding for agencies that were forced to curtail operations. However, it does not address the concerns of contractors and other workers who were not eligible for back pay.\n\nThe next funding deadline is January 30, and lawmakers are already beginning to negotiate the terms of the next spending package. The main sticking points are expected to be border security, immigration reform, and the future of the Affordable Care Act.\n\n"We're going to have to find a way to come together and find common ground," said Sen. Chris Murphy (D-Conn.). "The American people deserve a government that works for them, not one that is constantly on the brink of collapse."\n\nJordain Carney, Jennifer Scholtes, Katherine Tully-McManus and Connor O'Brien contributed to this report.	President Donald Trump signed legislation late Wednesday to end the government shutdown that spanned 43 days, punting the next funding deadline to January 30 and leaving Congress with a daunting to-do list for the new year.	2025-11-12 00:00:00	2025-12-02 14:03:47.442	2025-12-02 14:03:47.442	2025-12-02 14:03:47.442
86e8d676-0e6c-444d-89c7-94d8844e2c9c	https://www.politico.com/news/2025/11/13/trump-signs-bill-ending-government-shutdown-00650210	\N	dc72f70d-809a-42c1-b218-b2ed57239432	Trump signs bill ending government shutdown	President Donald Trump signed a bill Wednesday night to end the 43-day government shutdown, the longest in U.S. history. The House passed the bill by a 222-209 vote, with a handful of Democrats joining Republicans. The Senate passed the measure 68-32, after a 60-40 cloture vote. The bill funds the government through January 30 and bundles three full-year appropriations bills with the stopgap extension. The shutdown led to furloughs and delayed paychecks for hundreds of thousands of federal employees, and suspended or hampered numerous public services, including SNAP and WIC benefits. Research at federal agencies such as the NIH and CDC was delayed. Key entitlement programs like Social Security, Medicare, and Medicaid continued largely without interruption, but administrative support faced slowdowns. The government is now funded through January 30, but some projects and funding cycles will take weeks or months to recover fully.	President Donald Trump signed a bill Wednesday night to end the 43-day government shutdown, the longest in U.S. history. The House passed the bill by a 222-209 vote, with a handful of Democrats joinin...	2025-11-13 00:00:00	2025-12-02 14:03:47.455	2025-12-02 14:03:47.455	2025-12-02 14:03:47.455
