ompeii to a Rubens in a Paris Closet"},"content":{"rendered":"<p><span class=\\"drop\\">I<\\/span><span class=\\"small_caps\\">\\u2019m<\\/span> always chipper around Christmas and New Year\\u2019s, times of gift giving, snow, revelation, reflection, and renewal. Revelation isn\\u2019t just about prophets channeling God. I follow Biblical archaeology but also Greek and Roman discoveries, believing that there\\u2019s so much we don\\u2019t know, plus technology has advanced so much. AI, ground penetrating radar, drones, magnetology, and DNA science are a few of the new ways to marry technology to the human eye and touch and the scholarly mind and to read what\\u2019s underground. Egypt\\u2019s Valley of the Kings has been combed for hundreds of years, thousands counting tomb raiders, but just a few months ago archaeologists there confirmed they\\u2019d found the tomb of Thutmose II, who ruled during, we think, the 1480s <span class=\\"small_caps\\">b<span class=\\"small_caps\\">.c.<\\/span><\\/span> It\\u2019s the last missing tomb of an Eighteenth Dynasty pharaoh and the first discovered since King Tut\\u2019s tomb in 1922. The dynasty ran from around 1550 to 1292 <span class=\\"small_caps\\">b.c.<\\/span>\\u00a0and was ancient Egypt\\u2019s golden age.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1907153\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1907153\\" style=\\"width: 1839px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1907153 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?resize=620%2C405&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"405\\" data-image-id=\\"1907153\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?w=1839&amp;ssl=1 1839w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?resize=300%2C196&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?resize=552%2C360&amp;ssl=1 552w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?resize=1536%2C1002&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?resize=705%2C460&amp;ssl=1 705w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?resize=459%2C300&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?resize=634%2C414&amp;ssl=1 634w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?resize=883%2C576&amp;ssl=1 883w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?resize=1324%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1324w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?resize=50%2C33&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Pharaoh-Thutmose-II.jpg?w=1240&amp;ssl=1 1240w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1907153\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\">Fragments found in the tomb of Pharaoh Thutmose II. <cite>(Screenshot via History for GRANITE\\/YouTube)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>The evidence? The neighborhood\\u2019s right but the proof starts in the paint &#8212; a patch of ceiling painted blue with gold stars. The color and design are reserved for royal tombs. Scenes from the Book of the Dead reserved for pharaonic tombs had disintegrated into bits on the floor, though small passages are still on the walls. Good puzzle skills and AI reassembled them. An inscription on a fragment of an alabaster pot salutes \\u201cthe Son of Re, Thut,\\u201d but the rest hasn\\u2019t been found. There were several Thutmose pharaohs, but a fragment from another pot links the tomb to the husband of Hatshepsut, possibly the ruling queen in her own right, possibly any ninth Hatshepsut but we know of all the capo di capi called Thutmose, only Thutmose II had a wife named Hatshepsut. Thutmose\\u2019s daughter or granddaughter might have been the princess who discovered baby Moses in a basket, but a clique of Egyptologists and Biblical archaeologists think Thutmose II might have been Pharaoh during the Exodus. Timelines are sometimes set in sand, not stone.<\\/p>\\n<Connatix preset=\\"loggedOut\\"><\\/Connatix>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-1-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-1\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-1-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit><TopStories desktop=\\"true\\" variant=\\"default\\"><\\/TopStories>\\n<p>A joint British and Egyptian team is doing the excavation. Lots more needs to be done at the tomb, which seems to have been prone not to robbers but to flooding. It\\u2019s thought that its contents, including the king\\u2019s mummy, were moved not long after Thutmose\\u2019s burial. It\\u2019s all good fun to follow. I\\u2019m not focused on verifying Biblical events as much as on the art, artifact, and historical secrets the ground beneath our feet hides, sometimes after thousands of years.<\\/p>\\n<p>Egypt, its economy on life support, relies on tourism and baksheesh from Uncle Sam and a sheik here and there to forestall ruin. The government is spending a bundle to bring tourists back after the Arab Spring fiasco and terrorism fears. It just opened the new, billion-dollar Grand Egyptian Museum to show treasures of the pharaohs, among them Tutankhamun, or King Tut. Amenhotep III\\u2019s tomb opened in late October after a 20-year restoration. He ruled from the late 1380s or so to the early 1350s <span class=\\"small_caps\\">b.c.<\\/span> and was among the biggest enchiladas of the 18th Dynasty and thought to be the richest man in the world. The tomb was discovered by the French in 1799, already sacked. They documented and sealed it, everyone forgot about it, and it was rediscovered in 1915 but never restored until now. Its frescoes are now magnificent thanks to Japanese archaeologists and money and UNESCO, which does something good once a dynasty.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1907152\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1907152\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1907152 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?resize=620%2C362&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"362\\" data-image-id=\\"1907152\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?resize=617%2C360&amp;ssl=1 617w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/spa-pompeii.jpg?w=1240&amp;ssl=1 1240w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1907152\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\">Part of a newly discovered private home spa in Pompeii, buried under ash since 79 A.D. <cite>(Marco Cantile\\/LightRocket via Getty Images)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>I visited the archaeological parks at Pompeii and Paestum in late 2024 and have been following news there. What news can Pompeii possibly offer, destroyed in 79 <span class=\\"small_caps\\">a.d.<\\/span>\\u00a0by the eruption of Vesuvius and buried in as much as 30 feet of ash? A lot. Digging there started in the 1740s, but a third of the ancient city is still buried. In 2025, archaeologists showed the art press a sumptuous private home spa located in what was a 30-room house. With a boiler, hot and warm water rooms, and a cold plunge pool, it\\u2019s among the fanciest discoveries and in good shape owing to nearly 2,000 years sealed by ash. Walls are a vibrant red, floors are mosaic. The floor in the hot room is suspended over a cellar to which the boiler supplied sauna-standard air. Pipes and taps are intact. Slaves fed the fires to keep water hot. Hey, wasn\\u2019t slavery invented in America? Walking through Princeton University\\u2019s new art museum, which I\\u2019ll review on Saturday, you\\u2019d think so!<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1907149\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1907149\\" style=\\"width: 1706px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1907149 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Christ-on-the-Cross-by-Peter-Paul-Ruben.jpg?resize=620%2C436&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"436\\" data-image-id=\\"1907149\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Christ-on-the-Cross-by-Peter-Paul-Ruben.jpg?w=1706&amp;ssl=1 1706w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Christ-on-the-Cross-by-Peter-Paul-Ruben.jpg?resize=300%2C211&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Christ-on-the-Cross-by-Peter-Paul-Ruben.jpg?resize=512%2C360&amp;ssl=1 512w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Christ-on-the-Cross-by-Peter-Paul-Ruben.jpg?resize=1536%2C1080&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Christ-on-the-Cross-by-Peter-Paul-Ruben.jpg?resize=654%2C460&amp;ssl=1 654w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Christ-on-the-Cross-by-Peter-Paul-Ruben.jpg?resize=459%2C323&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Christ-on-the-Cross-by-Peter-Paul-Ruben.jpg?resize=589%2C414&amp;ssl=1 589w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Christ-on-the-Cross-by-Peter-Paul-Ruben.jpg?resize=819%2C576&amp;ssl=1 819w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Christ-on-the-Cross-by-Peter-Paul-Ruben.jpg?resize=1228%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1228w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Christ-on-the-Cross-by-Peter-Paul-Ruben.jpg?resize=50%2C35&amp;ssl=1 50w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1907149\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\"><i>Christ on the Cross<\\/i> by Peter Paul Rubens is displayed at the Osenat auction house in Fontainebleau, France, September 11, 2025. <cite>(Ian Langsdon\\/AFP via Getty Images)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>The Pompeii house, luxuriously fitted with a spa and an elegant dining room, must have been the resort town\\u2019s Mar-a-Lago. We think it was owned by a powerful Roman pol who vacationed in Pompeii. Parts of the complex seem to have been undergoing a reno since piles of construction supplies were in some of the rooms.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-2-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-small\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit><MoreInTag desktop=\\"true\\" variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreInTag>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-2-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-2\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit><TopStories mobile=\\"true\\" variant=\\"default\\"><\\/TopStories>\\n<p>We\\u2019re barely past Christmas, days before Epiphany, and months before Good Friday, so I\\u2019m getting ahead of myself, but the discovery &#8212; in a closet in a Paris townhouse &#8212; of <em>Christ on the Cross<\\/em> from 1613, painted by Peter Paul Rubens, is big news. The Paris auctioneer Jean-Pierre Osenat was cataloguing the house\\u2019s contents for an estate sale when he found what he remembered from an engraving as the long-lost painting by Rubens (1577-1640), probably done for an Antwerp patron, rare for Rubens in that Christ is dead as opposed to in agony and beseeching. At 41 by 29 inches, it\\u2019s not small. I looked in my closets today &#8212; hope springs eternal &#8212; and did find Bounty paper towels printed with scenes from Noah\\u2019s Ark, priceless to me but, alas, not a gusher.<\\/p>\\n<p>Nils B\\u00fcttner, the head of the Corpus Rubenianum and keeper of the flame, authenticated the picture through chemical analysis of the paint and the panel surface, paint handling, and enough X-rays to have killed Madame Curie. At the November 30 sale, it sold for \\u20ac2.3 million, around $2.7 million, on a \\u20ac1-2 million estimate. My spies tell me a very private English collector bought it. A discerning one, I\\u2019ll add.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1907148\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1907148\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1907148 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?resize=620%2C362&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"362\\" data-image-id=\\"1907148\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?resize=617%2C360&amp;ssl=1 617w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/kentucky-rifle.jpg?w=1240&amp;ssl=1 1240w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1907148\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\">Your art critic kicks off America 250 with a 1776 Kentucky rifle, held at the Inter Fair in New York, January 2025. <cite>(Photo, Jack Kontarinis)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>Readers, friends, and family ask me how I decide topics for my twice-a-week pieces. My interests are catholic and I like to be challenged. Plus I\\u2019m peripatetic and it\\u2019s a big country. I think I\\u2019m the only art critic &#8212; ever &#8212; committed to covering the visual arts in all 50 states. I hit Delaware, Jersey, Wisconsin, and New Mexico in 2025. Some exhibitions like <em>Sargent in Paris<\\/em> at the Met and <em>Rembrandt\\u2019s Time<\\/em> at the Norton in West Palm Beach are so good and sumptuous that I double dip, mellowed when it comes to art, verboten with a cracker at a reception. Starting this past July 4, I\\u2019ve focused on America 250 topics with stories on Mount Vernon, Colonial and Federal period spots in Boston, and the 250th anniversary of the U.S. Marines. I might have started my own work on the subject when I reviewed the Winter Show in New York in January 2025. Then, I held a 1776 Kentucky rifle for my fiercest photograph.<\\/p>\\n<Connatix preset=\\"legacy\\"><\\/Connatix>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-3-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit><MoreFromAuthor desktop variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreFromAuthor>\\n<p>Then there are openings like the Frick\\u2019s after a long redo. I love writing about the art market and art freshly for sale after decades, even centuries, in private hands.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-3-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-3\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit><MoreInTag mobile=\\"true\\" variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreInTag>\\n<p>I\\u2019m a Methodist &#8212; stern and rigorous &#8212; and not a Flagellant, so there are only so many bad exhibitions I can see and then review. There are so many ways to be bad, though. There was incoherence at the Amon Carter in Fort Worth, and bluster and bloviation &#8212; as well as incoherence &#8212; at the Smithsonian\\u2019s American Art Museum. There were one-trick ponies &#8212; racism rules the land &#8212; at Newark and Baltimore and, at the Met Gala, freak couture. There was the new, useless, and dishonest Climate Museum in New York. How can we as a nation survive without knowing how changes in the climate affect trans teens of color? The venerable Brooklyn Museum\\u2019s anchor 200th anniversary shows were touted &#8212; not by me &#8212; as \\u201cbold\\u201d but, as my very Tory grandmother would say, \\u201cBold means bad.\\u201d Then there\\u2019s bad, no, abysmal, psychotic governance. I\\u2019ve never known leadership as perverse as the Philadelphia Art Museum\\u2019s.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1907140\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1907140\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1907140 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?resize=620%2C362&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"362\\" data-image-id=\\"1907140\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?resize=617%2C360&amp;ssl=1 617w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/uss-texas.jpg?w=1240&amp;ssl=1 1240w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1907140\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\">The Battleship Texas Foundation will now receive its promised IMLS grant toward its renovation. USS <i>Texas<\\/i>, commissioned in 1912, was among the most lethal warships of its day. <cite>(Public domain\\/via Wikimedia)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>Then there\\u2019s the news, especially 2025\\u2019s art news from the Swamp. DOGE\\u2019s evisceration of the Institute of Museum and Library Services (IMLS) was the low point. Of the three core federal arts agencies &#8212; IMLS, the National Endowment for the Humanities (NEH), and the National Endowment for the Arts (NEA) &#8212; IMLS is the one that actually functioned at a high standard. Millions of dollars in good grants were rescinded willy-nilly only because they bore the stigma of Joe Biden\\u2019s senile, lowdown, lying rule. That the grants were eventually restored has to be counted as an embarrassment for the new order. I\\u2019m all for doing things chop chop, but very few IMLS grants have anything to do with fads.<\\/p>\\n<ArticleNewsletter desktop=\\"true\\"><\\/ArticleNewsletter><AdUnit name=\\"incontent-4-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-small\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>The three agencies might be forgotten, but don\\u2019t think they\\u2019re gone. Their billion dollars in congressional appropriations are still with us, about to be renewed by Congress. Is the money used as a slush fund for projects like the Heroes Sculpture Garden, a triumphal arch in Washington, or the American pavilion at the Venice Biennale? I have nothing against monuments, but we have too many. If there\\u2019s spare money, it ought to be spent restoring monuments desecrated during the Black Lives Matter miasma.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1907147\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1907147\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1907147 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?resize=620%2C362&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"362\\" data-image-id=\\"1907147\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?resize=617%2C360&amp;ssl=1 617w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/arch-trump.jpg?w=1240&amp;ssl=1 1240w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1907147\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\">President Donald Trump holds a model of an arch monument during a ballroom dinner in the East Room at the White House in Washington, D.C., October 15, 2025. <cite>(Jonathan Ernst\\/Reuters)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>That said, much as I believe in drill, baby, drill as a directive from God, I think build, baby, build &#8212; the new White House ballroom &#8212; might be too much of a good thing. A thousand seats? Won\\u2019t the hinterland tables reach Culpepper? I like bringing people to glitzy White House events, but less is more. A thousand-person event isn\\u2019t exclusive. There\\u2019s already been an architectural upheaval. Fusses over a ballroom, a heroes sculpture garden, a triumphal arch, a Nobel Peace Prize, the Ukraine cesspool, the ultimate money pit, and other sideshows not only detract but seem to mock the concerns of us simple country souls.<\\/p>\\n<ArticleNewsletter mobile=\\"true\\"><\\/ArticleNewsletter><AdUnit name=\\"incontent-4-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-4\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit><MoreFromAuthor mobile variant=\\"default\\"><\\/MoreFromAuthor>\\n<p>I\\u2019ve been to the Kennedy Center, now the Trump Kennedy Center, only a couple of times. After writing earlier this year that it was middlebrow, friends suggested otherwise, so I visited the next time I was in the Swamp. It\\u2019s the home of our very good National Symphony. It\\u2019s got the grandeur of a national performance hall. It can accommodate operas, plays, and symphonies, drawing great crowds, but having other, smaller-scale spaces can attract performances that can\\u2019t fill a big space, or can\\u2019t afford it, or aren\\u2019t suited for it.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-5-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-large\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>I don\\u2019t think public buildings should bear the name of anyone who\\u2019s still with us. We\\u2019re all sinners. Best to acknowledge a heroic person with a naming when that last breath extinguishes, more or less, the chance for sin and shame. I\\u2019d use the commemorative first-class stamp rule &#8212; three years after death, by which time wills are probated, bastards and bigamy exposed, and incriminating papers shredded. In ye olde North Haven in Connecticut, where I grew up, served on a dozen town boards, and learned the gold standards of good governance, no public buildings were named after anyone, living or dead.<\\/p>\\n<p>Federal cultural institutions, though, like the Smithsonian museums, tend to name additions, galleries, loading docks, curatorial positions, even bathrooms after the living since these things usually happen through philanthropy. The big-ticket items are, to be crass, sold after personal checks with lots of zeros at the end. President Trump has chaired the Kennedy Center board for all of eight months. I\\u2019m sure he was essential in Congress\\u2019s appropriation of $247 million in the Big Beautiful Bill to renovate the building.<\\/p>\\n<p>There\\u2019s a lawsuit now about whether or not the name change is legal. And the renovation hasn\\u2019t happened. The sudden name change seems indecorously timed. The statute naming the place the Kennedy Center seems to require congressional action to change it. I asked its press office whether or not the Big Beautiful Bill did so and haven\\u2019t heard back. Otherwise, when the left gets back in, and it will eventually, though I hope not in my lifetime, off to the smelter a chunk of the new name will go.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1907146\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1907146\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1907146 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?resize=620%2C362&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"362\\" data-image-id=\\"1907146\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?resize=617%2C360&amp;ssl=1 617w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/shape-of-power.jpg?w=1240&amp;ssl=1 1240w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1907146\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\">A recent Smithsonian American Art Museum exhibition shows an unseemly, repetitive, boring obsession with race. <cite>(Photo by Albert Ting, courtesy of the Smithsonian American Art Museum)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>I hope the impresarios and marquee names canceling performances get slapped with fat lawsuits. They\\u2019re punishing the public but mostly musicians, actors, and back-of-house people who won\\u2019t get paid.<\\/p>\\n<p>For months I heard nothing about the White House\\u2019s demands that the Smithsonian museums quit pushing Swamp prosecco politburo talking points. I\\u2019ve seen lots of Smithsonian exhibitions, mostly at the National Portrait Gallery, the American Art Museum, and the Renwick Gallery. Many are preachy, duplicitous, and redundant. The White House asked good questions about how Smithsonian programs evolve but, as I suggested might happen, the Smithsonian seemed to stonewall, hoping the head of the Office of Management and Budget (OMB), Russell Vought, would move on. I suspect Vought has a sophisticated tickler file with alert sounds more like the <em>William Tell<\\/em> Overture than wind chimes. A few days ago, Vought wrote asking for answers.<\\/p>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-6-desktop\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-small\\" unitType=\\"desktop\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<AdUnit name=\\"incontent-5-mobile\\" baseUnitName=\\"incontent-1\\" unitType=\\"mobile\\"><\\/AdUnit>\\n<p>Sometimes bureaucrats need the slap of unyielding, emphatic supervision. The Smithsonian isn\\u2019t independent. No part of the government, much less one getting a billion dollars a year from Congress, can prance la-di-da while it does what it wants.<\\/p>\\n<p>There are two searches now underway at the Smithsonian, one for the director of the National Portrait Gallery (NPG) and the other for the director of the American Art Museum. Left on its own, the masters of the Smithsonian will pick more of the same. I\\u2019d suggest the White House get involved, fast and hard.<\\/p>\\n<p>I\\u2019d also suggest OMB push the Smithsonian to sue the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art and artist Amy Sherald, and sue the heck out of \\u2019em, for pulling their splashy Sherald show from the NPG because the Smithsonian merely \\u201csuggested\\u201d Sherald\\u2019s full-length picture of a trans Statue of Liberty might be impolitic given the times and would certainly need enhanced interpretation. Sherald and SF MoMA left the Smithsonian with empty galleries a few weeks before it was slated to open. The NPG made Sherald famous when it commissioned her to paint Michelle Obama\\u2019s portrait. Where\\u2019s Roy Cohn when you need him?<\\/p>\\n<p>Six of the Smithsonian\\u2019s citizen trustees are either up for reappointment in 2026 or term limited and out the door in 2026. These trustees are picked by a joint congressional resolution. The place needs not a tsunami of a flush but a good housecleaning starting at the top. Congress and the White House need to get their ducks in order and a \\u201cquack, quack\\u201d alert won\\u2019t do for those tickler files to be activated. One with the \\u201cDun Dun, Dun Dun\\u201d tune from <em>Jaws<\\/em> seems right to me.<\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Some of 2025\\u2019s biggest art discoveries, plus the Swamp art scene spills into 2026.<\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"author":101,"featured_media":1907154,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"closed","sticky":false,"template"