height=\\"141\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/pharaohs-tomb.jpg?resize=252%2C141&amp;ssl=1\\" class=\\"attachment-loop-thumbnail size-loop-thumbnail\\" alt=\\"\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 252px) 100vw, 252px\\" decoding=\\"async\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/pharaohs-tomb.jpg?resize=252%2C141&amp;ssl=1 252w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/pharaohs-tomb.jpg?resize=160%2C90&amp;ssl=1 160w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/pharaohs-tomb.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/pharaohs-tomb.jpg?zoom=2&amp;resize=252%2C141&amp;ssl=1 504w\\" \\/>"},"source_url":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/pharaohs-tomb.jpg"},"featured_video":{"url":"","title":"","click_url":"","image_url":""},"issue":null,"series":null,"host":null,"primary_category":{"id":6917,"count":760,"description":"","link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/art\\/","name":"Art","slug":"art","taxonomy":"category","parent":1290,"meta":{"blog-section":false,"section-blog-ads":[]},"is_capital_matters_archive":false,"specific_term_link":"<a href=\\"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/art\\/\\" class=\\"underline\\">Art<\\/a>"},"primary_section":{"id":615,"count":86568,"description":"","link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/article\\/","name":"Article","slug":"article","taxonomy":"section","parent":0,"meta":{"blog-section":false,"section-blog-ads":[]},"is_capital_matters_archive":false},"primary_tag":{"id":5038,"count":342,"description":"","link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/tag\\/art\\/","name":"Art","slug":"art","taxonomy":"post_tag","meta":{"blog-section":false,"section-blog-ads":[]}},"subtitle":"Some of 2025\\u2019s biggest art discoveries, plus the Swamp art scene spills into 2026.","is_capital_matters":false,"is_wfb_100":false,"is_magazine_article":false,"is_premium_content":true,"table_of_contents":null,"truncated_content":"<p><span class=\\"drop\\">I<\\/span><span class=\\"small_caps\\">\\u2019m<\\/span> always chipper around Christmas and New Year\\u2019s, times of gift giving, snow, revelation, reflection, and renewal. Revelation isn\\u2019t just about prophets channeling God. I follow Biblical archaeology but also Greek and Roman discoveries, believing that there\\u2019s so much we don\\u2019t know, plus technology has advanced so much. AI, ground penetrating radar, drones, magnetology, and DNA science are a few of the new ways to marry technology to the human eye and touch and the scholarly mind and to read what\\u2019s underground. Egypt\\u2019s Valley of the Kings has been combed for hundreds of years, thousands counting tomb raiders, but just <span class=\\"small_caps\\"><\\/span> ...<\\/p>\\n","right_rail_widget":{"widget_type":"more-from-author","slideshow_widget_title":"Latest Photos","slideshow_widget_post":null,"slideshow_widget_post_title":"","slideshow_widget_categories_exclude":[6068]},"amp_enabled":true,"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/1906946","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/types\\/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/users\\/101"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/comments?post=1906946"}],"version-history":[{"count":6,"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/1906946\\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":1907457,"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/posts\\/1906946\\/revisions\\/1907457"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/media\\/1907154"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/media?parent=1906946"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/categories?post=1906946"},{"taxonomy":"post_tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/tags?post=1906946"},{"taxonomy":"section","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/section?post=1906946"},{"taxonomy":"author","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-json\\/wp\\/v2\\/coauthors?post=1906946"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\\/\\/api.w.org\\/{rel}","templated":true}]}},{"id":1905933,"date":"2025-12-27T06:30:05","date_gmt":"2025-12-27T11:30:05","guid":{"rendered":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/?p=1905933"},"modified":"2025-12-27T14:29:21","modified_gmt":"2025-12-27T19:29:21","slug":"art-in-the-new-year-wants-and-needs","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\\/\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/2025\\/12\\/art-in-the-new-year-wants-and-needs\\/","title":{"rendered":"New Year&#8217;s Wants, with a Nod to William Blake"},"content":{"rendered":"<p><span class=\\"drop\\">\\u2018I<\\/span><span class=\\"small_caps\\">want<\\/span>, I want\\u201d is William Blake\\u2019s 1793 philosophical cartoon showing a child reaching the moon not via a spaceship but a ladder. T. S. Eliot said Blake\\u2019s engraving, the size of a playing card, \\u201cexhibits the essential sickness or strength of the human soul\\u201d in a way children and adults alike can fathom.<\\/p>\\n<p>What do I want for 2026? Not a trip to the moon, I guarantee you. And \\u201cI want\\u201d seems very demanding, the English translation of my cat\\u2019s plaintive mews five minutes after her usual dinner time. Gravy trainers of all faiths, colors, creeds, and foreign origins use \\u201cgimme, gimme, gimme.\\u201d Polite and subtle as I am, I\\u2019d prefer \\u201cI\\u2019d like,\\u201d but some things I just want, and I want \\u2019em now.<\\/p>\\n<p>If I\\u2019m going to be on my feet in a museum for half a day, reading a hundred labels, I want to see fresh, edgy art and learn something. I want a 2026 Whitney Biennial that\\u2019s less about cant and more about quality. Last week, I read about the Whitney Museum of American Art\\u2019s just-announced roster of artists picked for its 82nd Biennial, opening on March 8. The Whitney Biennial is the longest-running survey show of American contemporary art. The 2026 show\\u2019s theme is \\u201cThe Strangeness of Our Times,\\u201d a tip of the hand to its vacuity. All times are strange. It\\u2019s a survey show in that it surveys the blinkered, juiceless concerns of the Manhattan curatorial class.<\\/p>\\n<p>A third of the artists say they\\u2019re queer. Half work either in New York or in California. A hefty, inexcusable 25 percent don\\u2019t live full-time in the United States. Isn\\u2019t the Biennial an <em>American<\\/em>\\u00a0art show? The Whitney tells us the artists are, collectively, from 25 states. I count twelve, among them Waawiyatanong, once called Detroit, in Michigan; the Navajo Nation; the Caddo Nation; and then there\\u2019s Hawaii, New Mexico, and Washington State. Tania Cruz is from New Haven, Connecticut, the city of my birth. Her work redefines black womanhood. No Appalachia, no Texas, no Kansas City, where everything might not be as \\u201cstrange\\u201d as the show\\u2019s curators think, but it\\u2019s the center of the country, and 2026 is the 250th anniversary of the country.<\\/p>\\n<p>Artist Ash Arder is \\u201cmotivated by the potential of empathy, curiosity, wonder, and play to slow the devastating effects of climate change.\\u201d Erin Jane Nelson explores the climate crisis \\u201cfrom a feminist perspective.\\u201d Two-thirds of the artists are under 45, one problem demographic after another. The matriarchy is so in, the patriarchy so out.<\\/p>\\n<p>The group selected for the Biennial \\u201cexamines,\\u201d its press statement says, \\u201cvarious forms of relationality, including interspecies kinships, familial relations, geopolitical entanglements, technological affinities, shared mythologies, and infrastructural supports.\\u201d I want jargon banished from the Whitney in 2026 \\u2014 and everywhere else, in perpetuity, if I\\u2019m allowed to be greedy. I\\u2019ll start by tweaking the Whitney\\u2019s press release for clarity.<\\/p>\\n<p>Taking the museum\\u2019s statement as an inscrutable draft, how about: \\u201cLooks at connections, sadly fractured, between lonely, awkward Millennials and their families who\\u2019ve discovered their preferred lifetime loves are fur babies or AI sex bots\\u201d? Geopolitical entanglements? Too vague. Let\\u2019s try: \\u201cWe\\u2019re codependent with Zionists, whom we hate along with anti-Semitic Jews, who are useful idiots but still Jews, and we\\u2019re entangled with Palestinians, too, whom we adore \\u2014 who doesn\\u2019t dream of a free Palestine, except the Jews \\u2014 but might not they toss the third of us who are queer off the tallest building in Gaza? Thankfully, Gaza\\u2019s flattened, but still, the angst and complexity!\\u201d Two of the Biennial artists are Palestinians, so I\\u2019m sure they\\u2019ll give us the skinny.<\\/p>\\n<p>Technological affinities? I\\u2019m low-tech. I doubt it means driving a standard shift. I doubt it means craftsmanship, mystery, and elasticity, either. Nothing says \\u201cshared mythologies\\u201d better than climate psychosis. Infrastructural supports? That\\u2019s just painting \\u201cBlack Lives Matter\\u201d on a crosswalk and believing it\\u2019s great art.<\\/p>\\n<p>Drew Sawyer, the Whitney photography curator, and Marcela Guerrero, the Latino art curator, did 300 studio visits, but they knew what they wanted and where they were going. Since this is America 250, I would have picked two or three artists from each of the 50 states chosen to reflect the abundance and breadth of American creativity. Go back to the drawing board, aim for a 50-state roster, ditch the foreigners, and see what you\\u2019ll find. And no climate art, please. It\\u2019s based on a scam and a fairy tale.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1905650\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1905650\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1905650 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?resize=620%2C362&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"362\\" data-image-id=\\"1905650\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?resize=617%2C360&amp;ssl=1 617w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/colors.jpg?w=1240&amp;ssl=1 1240w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1905650\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\">Burnt umber and cumulus cloud white vie for Colors of 2026. <cite>(Courtesy of Benjamin Moore and Pantone)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>\\u201cI want, I want.\\u201d I want Benjamin Moore and Pantone to agree on a 2026 Color of the Year, or at least on hue, luminance, and saturation, or at the very least two of the three. A few weeks ago, I wrote about Benjamin Moore\\u2019s choice, called Silhouette, a mix of burnt umber and charcoal. It\\u2019s moody and has some violet in it, but it isn\\u2019t schizophrenic. I think it\\u2019s manly and Victorian.<\\/p>\\n<p>Pantone\\u2019s Cloud Dancer \\u201coffers a promise of clarity,\\u201d the Pantone Color Institute tells us, \\u201ca conscious statement of simplification\\u201d given today\\u2019s polarization and hot tempers. I can\\u2019t say they\\u2019re wrong, and I respect the study of color. \\u201cPantone\\u2019s Color of the Year Sounds About White,\\u201d read one art news headline. Cloud Dancer is indeed white, and it\\u2019s the first time Pantone\\u2019s picked white. It\\u2019s easy for nitwits to believe race explains everything, so poor Pantone got tagged as a macro-aggressor and had to insist race actually had nothing to do with its choice. Last year, for example, Pantone picked Mocha Mousse. White\\u2019s the color of clean, puffy clouds, wedding dresses, and vanilla ice cream, all good things, but, as far as a setting for art, nothing looks good on a white wall except Jackson Pollock.<\\/p>\\n<p>I tried \\u201cI Want, I Want\\u201d against both Silhouette and Cloud Dancer, and neither suited, a bad sign since Blake tends to endure all colors. Our brilliant designer set Blake against Pantone\\u2019s Viva Magenta, its 2023 Color of the Year. It\\u2019s festive. It\\u2019s brave and fearless, too, good for a scene depicting a child reaching for the moon with a ladder.<\\/p>\\n<p>Benjamin Moore sells paint, so its colors aim at room decoration. Pantone\\u2019s colors affect makeup, flower arrangements, fashion, and advertising, as well as what to paint the parlor. Both go for zeitgeists, too. In picking Silhouette and Cloud Dancer, these companies have predicted two very different moods. Alas, mixing the two makes for boring beige.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1905647\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1905647\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1905647 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?resize=620%2C362&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"362\\" data-image-id=\\"1905647\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?resize=617%2C360&amp;ssl=1 617w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/Felix-Bracquemond.jpg?w=1240&amp;ssl=1 1240w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1905647\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\">Two of the most fabulous things on the art market in 2025. <b>Left<\\/b>: Faberg\\u00e9\\u2019s <i>Winter Egg<\\/i>, 1913. (Photo courtesy of Christie\\u2019s) <b>Right<\\/b>: F\\u00e9lix Bracquemond, <i>Portrait of \\u00c9douard Manet<\\/i>, 1864, pastel. (Public domain\\/via Wikimedia)<\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>\\u201cI want, I want.\\u201d I\\u2019m greedier and more demanding than I thought. Even after checking under the sofa cushions, I think I was still only in the one-octillionth range of the \\u00a322.9 million a lucky buyer paid for the Faberg\\u00e9 \\u201cWinter Egg.\\u201d That\\u2019s the piece from 1913 carved from rock crystal, packed with 4,500 diamonds, and seasoned with platinum, garnets, gold moss, and moonstone. It got the highest price ever paid for a Faberg\\u00e9 egg. Tsar Nicholas II commissioned it for his mother. I saw it in \\u201cFaberg\\u00e9: From Romance to Revolution\\u201d at the Victoria &amp; Albert Museum a few years ago and think about it as often as our dog thinks of food. It\\u2019s a little under six inches tall and proves that the best things come in small packages. Of all the hundreds, if not thousands, of things I\\u2019ve seen on the art market this year, that\\u2019s one I\\u2019d sell my grandmother\\u2019s rocking chair to buy.<\\/p>\\n<p>I write about historic art sales like Gustav Klimt\\u2019s\\u00a0<em>Portrait of Elisabeth Lederer<\\/em> from the mid-teens, which sold at Sotheby\\u2019s early this month for $236.4 million, the most ever paid at auction for a work of modern art. It\\u2019s a splashy thing, and I tend to like precious, intimate things (in part because we live in a farmhouse in Vermont with lots of bookshelves and limited wall space), though there\\u2019s always room for more art. I can dream, can\\u2019t I? F\\u00e9lix Bracquemond (1833\\u20131914) exhibited in the first Impressionist salon in 1874 and was a superb printmaker, ceramics designer, and tastemaker and close friend of Whistler and Manet. His 1864 pastel portrait of Manet (1832\\u20131883), which I saw at Agnew\\u2019s booth at the European Fine Art Fair, shows the young genius happy, serene, really, a <em>fl\\u00e2neur mensch<\\/em>. I loved it.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1905646\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1905646\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1905646 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?resize=620%2C362&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"362\\" data-image-id=\\"1905646\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?resize=617%2C360&amp;ssl=1 617w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/art-image.jpg?w=1240&amp;ssl=1 1240w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1905646\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\"><b>Left<\\/b>: Alma Allen (Photo by Ana Hop) <b>Right<\\/b>: Alma Allen, <i>Not Yet Titled<\\/i>, 2016, travertine. (Photo by Ed Mumford)<\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>I don\\u2019t feel I can demand anything from either Alma Allen (b. 1970), the artist representing the U.S. at the Venice Biennale starting in late April, or from the American Arts Conservancy (AAC), the sponsor of the American pavilion at the Venice show. I never heard of either, which is not extraordinary, as there are lots of artists and organizations I don\\u2019t know. That said, I\\u2019d at least heard of all of them but one going back to the 1960s, when educated Americans had a decent awareness of our mainstream contemporary art. Allen is an abstract sculptor from Utah living now in Mexico.<\\/p>\\n<p>AAC is a new organization. Its executive director and founder, Tampa-based Jenni Parido, owned a pet food supply business before starting AAC, which was launched just this year. Over the years, the State Department gave whichever group was handling the American pavilion at the Venice Biennale \\u2014 AAC this year \\u2014 $350,000. The deeply boring Simone Leigh pavilion in 2022 and Martin Puryear pavilion in 2024 cost, I\\u2019m told, more than $4 million. The Biennale artist\\u2019s dealer often leads the fundraising charge, but Allen\\u2019s dealer canned him after his selection. I don\\u2019t know why. I also don\\u2019t know anything about AAC\\u2019s fundraising model. I don\\u2019t know where millions in unspent National Endowment for the Arts, National Endowment for the Humanities, and Institute for Museum and Library Services dollars are going. Is some of it going to the American pavilion? Who\\u2019s selling Allen\\u2019s work now that he doesn\\u2019t have a dealer?<\\/p>\\n<p>Allen \\u2014 no relation, though the Mormons adopt us all post-death \\u2014 seems like a very good artist, but I\\u2019m not sure why or how he best represents the country, especially during America 250. I would have preferred a group exhibition showcasing American artists from different parts of the country working in different styles and media. Nonetheless, I\\u2019m looking forward to seeing Allen\\u2019s pavilion. My mind\\u2019s open.<\\/p>\\n<figure id=\\"attachment_1905732\\" aria-describedby=\\"caption-attachment-1905732\\" style=\\"width: 2057px\\" class=\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\"><img data-recalc-dims=\\"1\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" class=\\"wp-image-1905732 size-full\\" src=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?resize=620%2C362&#038;ssl=1\\" alt=\\"\\" width=\\"620\\" height=\\"362\\" data-image-id=\\"1905732\\" srcset=\\"https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?resize=300%2C175&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?resize=617%2C360&amp;ssl=1 617w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?resize=1536%2C896&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?resize=2048%2C1195&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?resize=789%2C460&amp;ssl=1 789w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?resize=459%2C268&amp;ssl=1 459w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?resize=710%2C414&amp;ssl=1 710w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?resize=987%2C576&amp;ssl=1 987w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?resize=1481%2C864&amp;ssl=1 1481w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?resize=50%2C29&amp;ssl=1 50w, https:\\/\\/i0.wp.com\\/www.nationalreview.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/12\\/The-golden-mask-of-Pharaoh-Tutankhamun.jpg?w=1240&amp;ssl=1 1240w\\" sizes=\\"auto, (max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px\\" \\/><figcaption id=\\"caption-attachment-1905732\\" class=\\"wp-caption-text\\">Front and back view of the golden mask of Pharaoh Tutankhamun inside the Grand Egyptian Museum. <cite>(<a href=\\"https:\\/\\/commons.wikimedia.org\\/wiki\\/File:The_golden_mask_of_Pharaoh_Tutankhamun_inside_the_Grand_Egyptian_Museum.jpg\\">\\u201cThe golden mask of Pharaoh Tutankhamun inside the Grand Egyptian Museum\\u201d<\\/a> by Egyptiankeng is licensed under <a href=\\"https:\\/\\/creativecommons.org\\/licenses\\/by-sa\\/4.0\\/deed.en\\">CC BY 4.0<\\/a>)<\\/cite><\\/figcaption><\\/figure>\\n<p>\\u201cI want, I want\\u201d in the worst way to visit the new Grand Egyptian Museum, just opened after 30 years of planning, construction, and delays due to war, terrorism fears, COVID, the Arab Spring, and just being Egyptian. It\\u2019s located by the Pyramids of Giza and displays nearly all the art found in King Tut\\u2019s tomb. I visited Cairo once, in 1979, as a college graduation present from my parents, but didn\\u2019t know much of anything then.<\\/p>\\n<p>I got to Luxor the day the freshly dethroned Shah did, flying direct as he did from Tehran to meet President Anwar Sadat and former President Gerald Ford, dispatched there by the earnest, naive, hubristic flop Jimmy Carter. All foreigners were evacuated from Luxor. Indiscriminately assigned a seat on the first plane to Cairo, I sat in first class, which was new to me.<\\/p>\\n<p>I saw the King Tut exhibition at the Met in the mid-1970s and experienced more of Tut in Cairo on my graduation trip, but the Grand Egyptian Museum is the museum project of the decade. I\\u2019ve got some nickels, dimes, quarters, and euros \\u2014 a fake currency, I know \\u2014 I found under the couch, and thanks to the One Big Beautiful Bill Act, my Social Security is mostly tax-free. Grandma\\u2019s rocking chair might bring $100. To misquote Scarlett O\\u2019Hara, \\u201cas God is my eyewitness, I\\u2019ll get to see Tut\\u2019s treasures again.\\u201d<\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Hoping for the best at two biennial shows, and will your art critic get to the new Grand Egyptian Museum?<\\/p>\\n","protected":false},"author":229,"featured_media":1905651,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"closed","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"hide-more-from