er pattern of genocidal actions.", "The debate over whether Israel's actions in Gaza constitute genocide is informed by statements and reports from international human rights organizations, media outlets, and comparisons to other historical genocides.", "Critics of the genocide label argue that the term requires a specific intent to destroy, in whole or in part, a national, ethnical, racial, or religious group, as per the United Nations Genocide Convention.", "Supporters of the genocide designation point to the high civilian death tolls, the destruction of infrastructure, and the impact on the Palestinian population in Gaza as evidence supporting their stance.", "The international community's views on Israel's actions in Gaza are diverse, with some countries and organizations condemning the actions as genocidal, while others caution against the use of the term without broad consensus."]	{"text": "Amnesty International has said Israel is “still committing genocide” against Palestinians in the Gaza Strip, despite the ceasefire agreed last month. 'The ceasefire risks creating a dangerous illusion that life in Gaza is returning to normal,' said Amnesty’s secretary general, Agnès Callamard. 'But while Israeli authorities and forces have reduced the scale of their attacks and allowed limited amounts of humanitarian aid into Gaza, the world must not be fooled. Israel’s genocide is not over.'", "articleId": "e47c8eca-11ef-4979-af3c-729e6596c3b6", "articleUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/nov/27/israel-still-committing-genocide-in-gaza-amnesty-international-says", "outletName": "The Guardian", "articleTitle": "Israel still committing genocide in Gaza, Amnesty International says"}
