rael.", "The debate includes perspectives from various sources, including news articles, judicial actions, and international bodies, indicating a complex and multifaceted discussion.", "Cultural and political figures, such as filmmakers and politicians, have also participated in the debate, using their platforms to express views on the situation in Gaza.", "The use of terms like 'genocidal war' by some media outlets reflects the intensity of the accusations and the strong language used in the debate.", "The impact of Israel's military operations in Gaza, including curfews, forced displacements, and lethal force, is a central point of contention in discussions about whether these actions constitute genocide."]	{"text": "The fate of one French judge is a case study in the west’s long unravelling. Nicolas Guillou cannot shop online. When he used Expedia to book a hotel in his own country, the reservation was cancelled within hours. He is “blacklisted by much of the world’s banking system”, unable to use most bank cards.", "articleId": "74508a9f-00bb-49d0-874b-58ca81ce09a7", "articleUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/nov/26/israel-international-law-donald-trump-us-sanction-judge", "outletName": "The Guardian", "articleTitle": "This French judge approved Netanyahu’s arrest warrant. Now Trump is targeting him"}
4933647b-420c-4478-87e1-56268be19f26	89804c2c-eb5b-4a70-86b7-b24df711c413	Unclear	20	0.5	1	2026-01-02 22:23:50.791	2026-01-02 21:24:14.952	2026-01-02 22:39:52.29	The verdict on whether harm reduction policies lead to lower rates of drug addiction is labeled as 'Unclear' due to the limited and conflicting evidence available from a single article. The article from an outlet with moderate credibility argues against the effectiveness of harm reduction policies, suggesting that such approaches have failed to stem the epidemic of drug addiction in certain areas. This stance is taken with high confidence. However, the overall verdict reflects significant uncertainty, primarily because the evidence comes from just one source, which does not provide a comprehensive overview of the topic. The lack of additional articles and perspectives contributes to the low confidence in the verdict and highlights the need for more research and diverse viewpoints to reach a more definitive conclusion.	2025-12-01 03:00:00	["Harm reduction policies aim to minimize the negative health, social, and legal impacts associated with drug use, without necessarily reducing drug consumption itself.", "Critics argue that harm reduction strategies, such as distributing drug supplies to addicts, may inadvertently normalize drug use and fail to address the root causes of addiction.", "Proponents of harm reduction assert that these policies can save lives by preventing overdose deaths and reducing the spread of infectious diseases among drug users.", "Some cities that initially embraced harm reduction policies are reconsidering their approach, citing concerns over continued high rates of drug addiction and public disorder.", "Research on the effectiveness of harm reduction policies is mixed, with some studies showing positive outcomes in reducing harm among drug users, while others question their impact on addiction rates.", "The debate includes considerations of ethical responsibility, with some viewing harm reduction as a compassionate approach to drug addiction, while others see it as enabling harmful behavior.", "Evaluating the success of harm reduction policies is complex, involving metrics beyond just addiction rates, including overdose deaths, disease transmission, and quality of life for drug users.", "The discussion around harm reduction versus more traditional drug control measures reflects broader societal debates on public health, individual freedom, and the role of government in addressing drug addiction."]	{"text": "At least two major West Coast cities are rethinking their so-called harm reduction policies that have sought to address addiction, signaling that these areas are overhauling their strategy to combat the drug crisis as addiction challenges persist. While cities including San Francisco and Seattle have previously adopted policies to distribute 'safer' drug supplies like clean foil and pipes that could be used to smoke fentanyl or other substances, these cities are now implementing a new approach that imposes new limitations on the distribution of these supplies.", "articleId": "db0a0d8c-310c-428e-8f89-4ef734f81f21", "articleUrl": "https://www.foxnews.com/politics/blue-cities-u-turn-distributing-drug-supplies-addicts-after-progressive-policies-fail-stem-epidemic", "outletName": "Fox News", "articleTitle": "Blue cities U-turn on distributing drug supplies to addicts after progressive policies fail to stem epidemic"}
